window.COURSE=[{"title": "Inside the computer", "goal": "Diagnose problems with evidence and recommend accessible hardware and software designs.", "lessons": [{"id": "w1d1", "week": 1, "day": 1, "title": "A system with a purpose", "standards": ["CS.D.8.a", "CS.HS.8.a"], "reading": ["A computing system combines hardware, the parts of a device, with software, the instructions that run on it. Input enters a system through a keyboard, touchpad, microphone, or sensor. A processor follows instructions. Memory holds information currently in use. Storage keeps files for later. Output leaves through a display, speaker, or another device. A network adapter connects the system to a network.", "A component is useful because of the task it supports. A larger screen may help a user read a diagram but make a device less portable. A microphone and speech input may help someone who cannot type easily. An improvement has a benefit, a limitation, and a user whose needs explain the choice. More expensive does not automatically mean better.", "Today you will investigate a fictional Chromebook. You will only change settings inside our model. Do not open a device, change school settings, install software, or enter personal information. A simulator leaves out details; it helps us reason about one part of a larger system."], "example": "A student needs to dictate a paragraph and save it. Input: microphone. Software: a permitted speech input tool. Processing: sound is converted to text. Memory: the draft currently open. Storage: the saved document. Output: displayed words. A headset might reduce background noise, but a student who cannot wear one needs another option.", "tasks": ["List input, processing, memory, storage, and output for writing a story on a Chromebook.", "Open Repair Lab case 1. Record the symptom and two possible causes before testing anything.", "Recommend one accessibility improvement for the story-writing task. Explain its benefit and tradeoff."], "exit": "Why does a microphone alone not complete the task of creating a saved text document?", "lab": "repair", "starter": "", "extension": ["Follow a request through a computer to understand why several components must cooperate. When you type a letter, an input device sends an event. The operating system and application interpret it, the processor executes instructions, working memory holds the active draft, and the display shows the result. Saving asks the system to retain the document in storage. Sending it to a cloud service adds a network step. A failure at one step can leave the others working, which is why a precise symptom matters.", "Startup is another sequence of cooperating parts. A desktop needs usable power before it can perform basic hardware checks. Firmware begins the startup process and locates a way to load the operating system, usually from storage. The operating system then manages devices and applications. A device with no power behaves differently from one that powers on but cannot load an operating system. Our virtual desktop case simplifies this process; it does not reproduce every manufacturer's startup codes or repair procedures.", "Use the scope of a failure to choose the next question. If one video is silent but other videos have sound, investigate that video's settings before claiming every speaker has failed. If all applications are slow, compare active workload and device resources. If one site fails while others work, investigate that service or page. None of these observations alone proves a diagnosis, but each makes some explanations more plausible and others less plausible.", "A recommendation should connect evidence to a user's task. Write the need, the relevant component or software feature, the proposed change, and a tradeoff. Then describe a test that could show whether the change helped. For example, larger text may improve reading, but the page still needs to fit the screen without hiding controls. Keyboard access may allow a user to complete a task that a mouse-only design prevents. Test the actual task, not merely whether a feature exists in a menu.", "A fair comparison keeps the task constant. If you compare two devices, ask both to complete the same task under similar conditions. Record time, errors, and accessibility barriers instead of relying on a general impression. If a user needs dictation, include a dictation task; if a user needs offline drafts, include a connection interruption. Describe the result in ordinary language so another person can repeat the check. A design decision becomes stronger when the evidence comes from the intended use. A quick successful trial still leaves questions about long-term reliability, so identify what you would test next before recommending a purchase or change."], "support": "Revisit this example: A student needs to dictate a paragraph and save it. Input: microphone. Software: a permitted speech input tool. Processing: sound is converted to text. Memory: the draft currently open. Storage: the saved document. Output: displayed words. A headset might reduce background noise, but a student who cannot wear one needs another option. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Recommend one accessibility improvement for the story-writing task. Explain its benefit and tradeoff. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w1d2", "week": 1, "day": 2, "title": "Evidence before a fix", "standards": ["CS.T.8.a"], "reading": ["Troubleshooting is a systematic investigation. First describe what you expected and what happened instead. Then gather evidence: what still works, when the problem began, and whether it happens in one app or several. Make a hypothesis, a possible explanation that can be tested. Choose a safe test that separates likely causes. Change one thing, observe the result, and decide what the result supports.", "A symptom is not a diagnosis. A silent video could result from a muted tab, low device volume, the wrong audio output, or a problem with the video. Replacing the computer before testing these explanations wastes time and gives little information. A good test is low risk and answers a specific question.", "Keep a repair log: symptom, hypothesis, test, observation, conclusion, and next action. A failed hypothesis can still be useful if the test rules out a cause. Stop and ask the teacher when a repair requires permissions, account access, physical repair, or changes outside the simulation."], "example": "Expected: sound from a video. Observed: no sound, but captions and picture work. Hypothesis: device volume is low. Test: inspect simulated volume. Observation: volume is 70%. Conclusion: low device volume is unlikely. Next: inspect tab mute. This sequence is more informative than clicking every fix at once.", "tasks": ["For Repair Lab case 1, write two hypotheses and predict an observation for each.", "Run at least two diagnostic tests. Record the results exactly and identify a cause each result rules out.", "Choose one fix supported by evidence. Verify by testing playback again. Explain why your first guess was or was not supported."], "exit": "Why should you change one variable at a time?", "lab": "repair", "starter": "", "extension": ["Follow a request through a computer to understand why several components must cooperate. When you type a letter, an input device sends an event. The operating system and application interpret it, the processor executes instructions, working memory holds the active draft, and the display shows the result. Saving asks the system to retain the document in storage. Sending it to a cloud service adds a network step. A failure at one step can leave the others working, which is why a precise symptom matters.", "Startup is another sequence of cooperating parts. A desktop needs usable power before it can perform basic hardware checks. Firmware begins the startup process and locates a way to load the operating system, usually from storage. The operating system then manages devices and applications. A device with no power behaves differently from one that powers on but cannot load an operating system. Our virtual desktop case simplifies this process; it does not reproduce every manufacturer's startup codes or repair procedures.", "Use the scope of a failure to choose the next question. If one video is silent but other videos have sound, investigate that video's settings before claiming every speaker has failed. If all applications are slow, compare active workload and device resources. If one site fails while others work, investigate that service or page. None of these observations alone proves a diagnosis, but each makes some explanations more plausible and others less plausible.", "A recommendation should connect evidence to a user's task. Write the need, the relevant component or software feature, the proposed change, and a tradeoff. Then describe a test that could show whether the change helped. For example, larger text may improve reading, but the page still needs to fit the screen without hiding controls. Keyboard access may allow a user to complete a task that a mouse-only design prevents. Test the actual task, not merely whether a feature exists in a menu.", "A fair comparison keeps the task constant. If you compare two devices, ask both to complete the same task under similar conditions. Record time, errors, and accessibility barriers instead of relying on a general impression. If a user needs dictation, include a dictation task; if a user needs offline drafts, include a connection interruption. Describe the result in ordinary language so another person can repeat the check. A design decision becomes stronger when the evidence comes from the intended use. A quick successful trial still leaves questions about long-term reliability, so identify what you would test next before recommending a purchase or change."], "support": "Revisit this example: Expected: sound from a video. Observed: no sound, but captions and picture work. Hypothesis: device volume is low. Test: inspect simulated volume. Observation: volume is 70%. Conclusion: low device volume is unlikely. Next: inspect tab mute. This sequence is more informative than clicking every fix at once. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Choose one fix supported by evidence. Verify by testing playback again. Explain why your first guess was or was not supported. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w1d3", "week": 1, "day": 3, "title": "Memory storage and slowdowns", "standards": ["CS.D.8.a", "CS.T.8.a", "DA.DCS.8.b"], "reading": ["Memory and storage solve different problems. Working memory holds active instructions and data while apps run. Storage retains saved files after an app closes or the device shuts down. A file can be safely stored even when there is not enough working memory to open many apps at once. Unsaved work in an app is not the same as a saved file.", "Slow performance has several possible causes. Many open tabs may compete for working memory and processor time. A network interruption can make a website wait even when the computer works normally. An app problem may affect only one tab. A slow device alone is not proof of malware. Evidence about other apps and connection status helps narrow the explanation.", "Use safe responses: save work, close unneeded tabs, retry an allowed task, or ask for help. Never delete another person's files or use a factory reset as an early troubleshooting step. A useful device recommendation compares the user's task with both device capability and constraints such as cost, portability, accessibility, and school permissions."], "example": "A writing app responds quickly but a streaming video buffers. Closing tabs might not address the cause. Test the simulated connection first. In a different case, many tabs are open and every app responds slowly while the connection is good; closing unneeded tabs after saving work is a reasonable test.", "tasks": ["In Repair Lab case 2, compare tab load and connection evidence. Predict which test will distinguish memory pressure from network delay.", "Run the tests and fix. Compare this cause with the silent-video cause.", "Create a two-column comparison of working memory and storage. Recommend an improvement for a student who needs several learning apps open."], "exit": "A saved file remains after shutdown. Does that show working memory or storage? Explain.", "lab": "repair", "starter": "", "extension": ["Follow a request through a computer to understand why several components must cooperate. When you type a letter, an input device sends an event. The operating system and application interpret it, the processor executes instructions, working memory holds the active draft, and the display shows the result. Saving asks the system to retain the document in storage. Sending it to a cloud service adds a network step. A failure at one step can leave the others working, which is why a precise symptom matters.", "Startup is another sequence of cooperating parts. A desktop needs usable power before it can perform basic hardware checks. Firmware begins the startup process and locates a way to load the operating system, usually from storage. The operating system then manages devices and applications. A device with no power behaves differently from one that powers on but cannot load an operating system. Our virtual desktop case simplifies this process; it does not reproduce every manufacturer's startup codes or repair procedures.", "Use the scope of a failure to choose the next question. If one video is silent but other videos have sound, investigate that video's settings before claiming every speaker has failed. If all applications are slow, compare active workload and device resources. If one site fails while others work, investigate that service or page. None of these observations alone proves a diagnosis, but each makes some explanations more plausible and others less plausible.", "A recommendation should connect evidence to a user's task. Write the need, the relevant component or software feature, the proposed change, and a tradeoff. Then describe a test that could show whether the change helped. For example, larger text may improve reading, but the page still needs to fit the screen without hiding controls. Keyboard access may allow a user to complete a task that a mouse-only design prevents. Test the actual task, not merely whether a feature exists in a menu.", "A fair comparison keeps the task constant. If you compare two devices, ask both to complete the same task under similar conditions. Record time, errors, and accessibility barriers instead of relying on a general impression. If a user needs dictation, include a dictation task; if a user needs offline drafts, include a connection interruption. Describe the result in ordinary language so another person can repeat the check. A design decision becomes stronger when the evidence comes from the intended use. A quick successful trial still leaves questions about long-term reliability, so identify what you would test next before recommending a purchase or change."], "support": "Revisit this example: A writing app responds quickly but a streaming video buffers. Closing tabs might not address the cause. Test the simulated connection first. In a different case, many tabs are open and every app responds slowly while the connection is good; closing unneeded tabs after saving work is a reasonable test. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Create a two-column comparison of working memory and storage. Recommend an improvement for a student who needs several learning apps open. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w1d4", "week": 1, "day": 4, "title": "Design a better learning device", "standards": ["CS.D.8.a", "CS.HS.8.a", "IC.Cu.8.b"], "reading": ["Design begins with a user's task and constraints. A requirement is something the solution must do. A constraint limits the choices, such as a budget, battery life, permitted software, or available connection. A design combines components so information can enter, be processed, be stored, and produce useful output. A list of impressive parts is not yet a design.", "Accessibility means people with different needs can use a system. Keyboard access helps someone who cannot use a mouse. Captions help users who cannot hear audio and users in a quiet room. Readable contrast and text size help many readers. Avoid assuming one adaptation works for everyone. Ask the user or use the needs described in the scenario.", "Evaluate a design with a test tied to a requirement. If the requirement is keyboard access, complete the task without using a mouse. Document the result, revise one feature, and test again. Your design can be a digital diagram or written model; no physical construction is needed."], "example": "User: a student must read directions, dictate an answer, and submit it with an unreliable connection. Design: readable browser page + microphone + permitted dictation + local draft export + Classroom upload when connected. Test: can the student export a draft before disconnecting? Tradeoff: submission still needs a connection.", "tasks": ["Design a browser learning station for a user who needs large text, keyboard access, and saved drafts. Diagram hardware and software connections using text arrows.", "Compare two alternatives using cost, accessibility, and reliability. Select one with evidence.", "Ask a partner to review the diagram digitally or complete the provided user-role review yourself. Revise one feature and describe a test."], "exit": "What requirement did your revision address, and how would you verify it?", "lab": "repair", "starter": "", "extension": ["Follow a request through a computer to understand why several components must cooperate. When you type a letter, an input device sends an event. The operating system and application interpret it, the processor executes instructions, working memory holds the active draft, and the display shows the result. Saving asks the system to retain the document in storage. Sending it to a cloud service adds a network step. A failure at one step can leave the others working, which is why a precise symptom matters.", "Startup is another sequence of cooperating parts. A desktop needs usable power before it can perform basic hardware checks. Firmware begins the startup process and locates a way to load the operating system, usually from storage. The operating system then manages devices and applications. A device with no power behaves differently from one that powers on but cannot load an operating system. Our virtual desktop case simplifies this process; it does not reproduce every manufacturer's startup codes or repair procedures.", "Use the scope of a failure to choose the next question. If one video is silent but other videos have sound, investigate that video's settings before claiming every speaker has failed. If all applications are slow, compare active workload and device resources. If one site fails while others work, investigate that service or page. None of these observations alone proves a diagnosis, but each makes some explanations more plausible and others less plausible.", "A recommendation should connect evidence to a user's task. Write the need, the relevant component or software feature, the proposed change, and a tradeoff. Then describe a test that could show whether the change helped. For example, larger text may improve reading, but the page still needs to fit the screen without hiding controls. Keyboard access may allow a user to complete a task that a mouse-only design prevents. Test the actual task, not merely whether a feature exists in a menu.", "A fair comparison keeps the task constant. If you compare two devices, ask both to complete the same task under similar conditions. Record time, errors, and accessibility barriers instead of relying on a general impression. If a user needs dictation, include a dictation task; if a user needs offline drafts, include a connection interruption. Describe the result in ordinary language so another person can repeat the check. A design decision becomes stronger when the evidence comes from the intended use. A quick successful trial still leaves questions about long-term reliability, so identify what you would test next before recommending a purchase or change."], "support": "Revisit this example: User: a student must read directions, dictate an answer, and submit it with an unreliable connection. Design: readable browser page + microphone + permitted dictation + local draft export + Classroom upload when connected. Test: can the student export a draft before disconnecting? Tradeoff: submission still needs a connection. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Ask a partner to review the diagram digitally or complete the provided user-role review yourself. Revise one feature and describe a test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w1d5", "week": 1, "day": 5, "title": "The help desk challenge", "standards": ["CS.T.8.a", "CS.HS.8.a", "CS.D.8.a"], "reading": ["A help desk report lets another person reproduce a problem and understand why a solution was selected. Write the expected behavior, observed symptom, relevant evidence, tests in order, and verification result. Separate observations from explanations. “The model shows the tab is muted” is evidence. “The speaker is broken” is a hypothesis until supported by a test.", "A good report also states limits. Our repair model has a small set of causes and does not represent every real Chromebook failure. If a repair does not work in the real classroom, preserve work and contact the teacher rather than changing restricted settings. Communicating a limit makes a technical explanation more trustworthy.", "Today you will complete an independent repair case, improve a design recommendation, and take the Week 1 assessment assigned by your teacher. Use the first part of class to create your evidence. Submit your response file or copy your responses into your assigned Google Classroom document. A local save does not turn in your assignment."], "example": "Report excerpt: Other websites load, but this page reports an error. The connection test passes, so I tested a different page. It loads. I refreshed the affected page in the model and it recovered. I verified the page displayed content. I would ask for help if refreshing did not work; I would not reset the device.", "tasks": ["Complete Repair Lab case 3 independently. Record at least two tests, your conclusion, and verification.", "Write a help desk response that another student could follow. Include one model limitation.", "Revise yesterday's device recommendation using one test result. Export your Week 1 work, submit it in Classroom, and complete the teacher-assigned Week 1 quiz."], "exit": "Which observation most strongly supported your repair decision, and why?", "lab": "repair", "starter": "", "extension": ["Follow a request through a computer to understand why several components must cooperate. When you type a letter, an input device sends an event. The operating system and application interpret it, the processor executes instructions, working memory holds the active draft, and the display shows the result. Saving asks the system to retain the document in storage. Sending it to a cloud service adds a network step. A failure at one step can leave the others working, which is why a precise symptom matters.", "Startup is another sequence of cooperating parts. A desktop needs usable power before it can perform basic hardware checks. Firmware begins the startup process and locates a way to load the operating system, usually from storage. The operating system then manages devices and applications. A device with no power behaves differently from one that powers on but cannot load an operating system. Our virtual desktop case simplifies this process; it does not reproduce every manufacturer's startup codes or repair procedures.", "Use the scope of a failure to choose the next question. If one video is silent but other videos have sound, investigate that video's settings before claiming every speaker has failed. If all applications are slow, compare active workload and device resources. If one site fails while others work, investigate that service or page. None of these observations alone proves a diagnosis, but each makes some explanations more plausible and others less plausible.", "A recommendation should connect evidence to a user's task. Write the need, the relevant component or software feature, the proposed change, and a tradeoff. Then describe a test that could show whether the change helped. For example, larger text may improve reading, but the page still needs to fit the screen without hiding controls. Keyboard access may allow a user to complete a task that a mouse-only design prevents. Test the actual task, not merely whether a feature exists in a menu.", "A fair comparison keeps the task constant. If you compare two devices, ask both to complete the same task under similar conditions. Record time, errors, and accessibility barriers instead of relying on a general impression. If a user needs dictation, include a dictation task; if a user needs offline drafts, include a connection interruption. Describe the result in ordinary language so another person can repeat the check. A design decision becomes stronger when the evidence comes from the intended use. A quick successful trial still leaves questions about long-term reliability, so identify what you would test next before recommending a purchase or change."], "support": "Revisit this example: Report excerpt: Other websites load, but this page reports an error. The connection test passes, so I tested a different page. It loads. I refreshed the affected page in the model and it recovered. I verified the page displayed content. I would ask for help if refreshing did not work; I would not reset the device. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Revise yesterday's device recommendation using one test result. Export your Week 1 work, submit it in Classroom, and complete the teacher-assigned Week 1 quiz. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Networks and connected things", "goal": "Model network hardware, protocols, packet loss, and an IoT information lifecycle.", "lessons": [{"id": "w2d1", "week": 2, "day": 1, "title": "Where does a web request go?", "standards": ["NI.N.8.a"], "reading": ["A network connects computing devices so they can exchange information. Your Chromebook is a client when it asks a server for a lesson. A server is another computer that responds to requests. A cloud server is still a physical computer, usually in a managed data center. The word cloud does not mean information is stored in the air.", "A wireless access point connects wireless devices to a local network. A switch moves traffic among devices on that local network. A router forwards packets between networks. One box may perform several roles, so a classroom diagram should label functions rather than assume every function requires a separate box.", "Data travels through several links. A working Wi-Fi connection does not prove that the school has a working internet connection. A problem at a server may affect one service while other services work. Use a model to identify which part of a path could explain the evidence."], "example": "A Chromebook can open a local school page but not a distant cloud page. The local connection is working for that request. The failure could be farther along the path or at the destination. It does not prove the Chromebook keyboard is faulty.", "tasks": ["Draw a labeled path from Chromebook to cloud server using text arrows. Explain each component's role.", "Use Network Lab to send a message. Record its pieces and reconstruction.", "Remove one component from your diagram. Explain which communication stops and which local tasks might still work."], "exit": "Why is a cloud server part of the physical world?", "lab": "network", "starter": "", "extension": ["Trace an information request in both directions. The client sends a request toward a server, and the server sends a response back through network links. The response need not travel through exactly the same sequence of routers in a real network. Our diagram uses a single simplified path to make the roles visible. A connection between two points can work while another part fails, so a useful test identifies which portion it exercised.", "Addresses help systems deliver information to a destination. People usually type a readable domain name; a name-resolution service helps locate the corresponding network address. A domain name, an address, and a page's content are different things. A correct address does not prove that the content is reliable. Similarly, an encrypted connection protects communication on the path but does not decide whether the person operating the destination is honest.", "Reliable communication requires more than sending data once. A receiving system may need to recognize order, acknowledge receipt, detect missing information, and obtain it again. These activities consume time and capacity. A live application may make a different tradeoff from a file transfer because an old update can be less useful than a fresh one. Explain the application's goal before judging a network strategy.", "When drawing a network model, label each arrow with what moves along it and each node with its role. Include where information is gathered, received, stored, and analyzed. Add a failure condition and a safe response. A temperature alert design might preserve recent readings locally, label stale data clearly, and ask a person to check conditions when fresh readings stop. That plan connects the networking model to a human decision instead of treating successful delivery as the only goal.", "A useful network test has a defined starting point and destination. Write what you sent, where it was supposed to go, and what arrived. If a message is incomplete, distinguish a missing piece from a piece received in the wrong order. Both require interpretation rules, but the evidence differs. A diagram with numbered pieces and acknowledgments helps a reader follow the process without guessing. When a service resumes after a retry, report the successful recovery and the extra step required. That evidence supports a reliability claim within the model while leaving real-world timing and capacity questions open for future investigation."], "support": "Revisit this example: A Chromebook can open a local school page but not a distant cloud page. The local connection is working for that request. The failure could be farther along the path or at the destination. It does not prove the Chromebook keyboard is faulty. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Remove one component from your diagram. Explain which communication stops and which local tasks might still work. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w2d2", "week": 2, "day": 2, "title": "Rules make communication possible", "standards": ["NI.N.8.b"], "reading": ["A protocol is an agreed set of rules for communication. Devices must agree on how to format, address, send, receive, and interpret information. Protocols work in layers. A web browser uses web rules while lower layers move data through networks.", "HTTP describes web requests and responses. HTTPS uses TLS to protect web communication in transit. Encryption helps prevent outsiders from reading data on the path. A deceptive site can still use HTTPS, so a secure connection does not establish that the site's claims are true. IMAP lets a mail client access and synchronize messages on a server. POP3 retrieves messages; whether a server copy remains depends on configuration.", "IP addresses and routes packets between networks. TCP provides a reliable ordered stream and can retransmit lost information. These roles differ from a website's content. You do not need to memorize packet headers today; you do need to explain why two computers cannot exchange meaningful messages without compatible rules."], "example": "Two people exchange numbers but disagree about whether the first number is a page number or a score. They receive the same symbols but interpret them differently. A shared format fixes this communication problem. Internet protocols serve a similar coordinating purpose.", "tasks": ["Inspect all five protocol explanations in Network Lab. Make a purpose-and-example table.", "Design a three-rule protocol for sending a fictional three-part message, including ordering and acknowledgment.", "Exchange a message digitally with a partner, or test your own sender and receiver roles. Show one failure caused by breaking a rule and revise the rule."], "exit": "What does HTTPS protect, and what does it not prove?", "lab": "network", "starter": "", "extension": ["Trace an information request in both directions. The client sends a request toward a server, and the server sends a response back through network links. The response need not travel through exactly the same sequence of routers in a real network. Our diagram uses a single simplified path to make the roles visible. A connection between two points can work while another part fails, so a useful test identifies which portion it exercised.", "Addresses help systems deliver information to a destination. People usually type a readable domain name; a name-resolution service helps locate the corresponding network address. A domain name, an address, and a page's content are different things. A correct address does not prove that the content is reliable. Similarly, an encrypted connection protects communication on the path but does not decide whether the person operating the destination is honest.", "Reliable communication requires more than sending data once. A receiving system may need to recognize order, acknowledge receipt, detect missing information, and obtain it again. These activities consume time and capacity. A live application may make a different tradeoff from a file transfer because an old update can be less useful than a fresh one. Explain the application's goal before judging a network strategy.", "When drawing a network model, label each arrow with what moves along it and each node with its role. Include where information is gathered, received, stored, and analyzed. Add a failure condition and a safe response. A temperature alert design might preserve recent readings locally, label stale data clearly, and ask a person to check conditions when fresh readings stop. That plan connects the networking model to a human decision instead of treating successful delivery as the only goal.", "A useful network test has a defined starting point and destination. Write what you sent, where it was supposed to go, and what arrived. If a message is incomplete, distinguish a missing piece from a piece received in the wrong order. Both require interpretation rules, but the evidence differs. A diagram with numbered pieces and acknowledgments helps a reader follow the process without guessing. When a service resumes after a retry, report the successful recovery and the extra step required. That evidence supports a reliability claim within the model while leaving real-world timing and capacity questions open for future investigation."], "support": "Revisit this example: Two people exchange numbers but disagree about whether the first number is a page number or a score. They receive the same symbols but interpret them differently. A shared format fixes this communication problem. Internet protocols serve a similar coordinating purpose. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Exchange a message digitally with a partner, or test your own sender and receiver roles. Show one failure caused by breaking a rule and revise the rule. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w2d3", "week": 2, "day": 3, "title": "When a packet goes missing", "standards": ["NI.N.8.c", "DA.IM.8.a"], "reading": ["A large message can be divided into smaller pieces called packets. Networks may delay, lose, or reorder packets. A receiver needs rules to interpret what arrives. In a reliable delivery model, the system detects missing information and retransmits it. Retrying can make the message complete but adds delay.", "Not every application uses the same delivery strategy. A live voice or game application may prioritize timely updates instead of waiting for every missing piece. Some applications add their own recovery rules. A simple contrast between retry and no retry is useful, but real networks include many additional mechanisms.", "A simulation is a model with stated assumptions. Our model sends three pieces and lets you choose one lost piece. It does not model congestion, changing routes, or every protocol feature. A claim such as retry always makes communication faster is contradicted by the extra work of sending again. Measure completeness and delay as separate outcomes."], "example": "LEARN, BUILD, SHARE arrives as LEARN, missing, SHARE. With reliable ordered delivery the receiver identifies the gap and obtains BUILD again before reconstructing the message. Without recovery the receiver has incomplete content; it cannot infer every lost word safely.", "tasks": ["Predict the outputs for four combinations of loss and delivery mode. Use Network Lab to test them.", "Record completeness and whether a retry was needed. Explain the cost of recovery.", "Write a claim supported by all four trials and one claim the model cannot support."], "exit": "Why can retransmission improve completeness while adding delay?", "lab": "network", "starter": "", "extension": ["Trace an information request in both directions. The client sends a request toward a server, and the server sends a response back through network links. The response need not travel through exactly the same sequence of routers in a real network. Our diagram uses a single simplified path to make the roles visible. A connection between two points can work while another part fails, so a useful test identifies which portion it exercised.", "Addresses help systems deliver information to a destination. People usually type a readable domain name; a name-resolution service helps locate the corresponding network address. A domain name, an address, and a page's content are different things. A correct address does not prove that the content is reliable. Similarly, an encrypted connection protects communication on the path but does not decide whether the person operating the destination is honest.", "Reliable communication requires more than sending data once. A receiving system may need to recognize order, acknowledge receipt, detect missing information, and obtain it again. These activities consume time and capacity. A live application may make a different tradeoff from a file transfer because an old update can be less useful than a fresh one. Explain the application's goal before judging a network strategy.", "When drawing a network model, label each arrow with what moves along it and each node with its role. Include where information is gathered, received, stored, and analyzed. Add a failure condition and a safe response. A temperature alert design might preserve recent readings locally, label stale data clearly, and ask a person to check conditions when fresh readings stop. That plan connects the networking model to a human decision instead of treating successful delivery as the only goal.", "A useful network test has a defined starting point and destination. Write what you sent, where it was supposed to go, and what arrived. If a message is incomplete, distinguish a missing piece from a piece received in the wrong order. Both require interpretation rules, but the evidence differs. A diagram with numbered pieces and acknowledgments helps a reader follow the process without guessing. When a service resumes after a retry, report the successful recovery and the extra step required. That evidence supports a reliability claim within the model while leaving real-world timing and capacity questions open for future investigation."], "support": "Revisit this example: LEARN, BUILD, SHARE arrives as LEARN, missing, SHARE. With reliable ordered delivery the receiver identifies the gap and obtains BUILD again before reconstructing the message. Without recovery the receiver has incomplete content; it cannot infer every lost word safely. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write a claim supported by all four trials and one claim the model cannot support. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w2d4", "week": 2, "day": 4, "title": "A virtual smart greenhouse", "standards": ["NI.IOT.8.a", "NI.IOT.8.b", "CS.HS.8.a"], "reading": ["The Internet of Things connects devices that sense or act on the world. A smart greenhouse might gather temperature from a sensor, transmit measurements through a network, receive them at a server, and analyze the values to decide whether to notify a grower. A sensor alone does not make a complete information system.", "A lifecycle diagram shows gathering, transmission, reception, and analysis. Add a return path if software controls an actuator such as a fan. Include a human decision when an automatic action could cause harm. If the connection fails, the design needs a safe fallback rather than assuming every reading arrives immediately.", "Careers have different responsibilities in this system. An embedded developer writes device software, a network specialist manages communication, a data analyst investigates readings, and a security specialist helps protect access. Work overlaps, and team members must explain their decisions to people with different expertise."], "example": "At minute 4 a fictional sensor reports 25 degrees Celsius. The device sends the reading, the server receives it, and a rule flags values above 24. The flag is an output of analysis. It is not evidence that a real fan has switched on.", "tasks": ["Use Data Lab's fictional temperatures. Diagram all four lifecycle stages and a proposed fan response.", "Choose a threshold, test readings below, at, and above it, and specify what happens if data is missing.", "Compare two IoT careers: tasks, required skills, and what each contributes to this greenhouse. Use the reading as a source and identify one question for future research."], "exit": "Which stage turns a received reading into an alert?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["Trace an information request in both directions. The client sends a request toward a server, and the server sends a response back through network links. The response need not travel through exactly the same sequence of routers in a real network. Our diagram uses a single simplified path to make the roles visible. A connection between two points can work while another part fails, so a useful test identifies which portion it exercised.", "Addresses help systems deliver information to a destination. People usually type a readable domain name; a name-resolution service helps locate the corresponding network address. A domain name, an address, and a page's content are different things. A correct address does not prove that the content is reliable. Similarly, an encrypted connection protects communication on the path but does not decide whether the person operating the destination is honest.", "Reliable communication requires more than sending data once. A receiving system may need to recognize order, acknowledge receipt, detect missing information, and obtain it again. These activities consume time and capacity. A live application may make a different tradeoff from a file transfer because an old update can be less useful than a fresh one. Explain the application's goal before judging a network strategy.", "When drawing a network model, label each arrow with what moves along it and each node with its role. Include where information is gathered, received, stored, and analyzed. Add a failure condition and a safe response. A temperature alert design might preserve recent readings locally, label stale data clearly, and ask a person to check conditions when fresh readings stop. That plan connects the networking model to a human decision instead of treating successful delivery as the only goal.", "A useful network test has a defined starting point and destination. Write what you sent, where it was supposed to go, and what arrived. If a message is incomplete, distinguish a missing piece from a piece received in the wrong order. Both require interpretation rules, but the evidence differs. A diagram with numbered pieces and acknowledgments helps a reader follow the process without guessing. When a service resumes after a retry, report the successful recovery and the extra step required. That evidence supports a reliability claim within the model while leaving real-world timing and capacity questions open for future investigation."], "support": "Revisit this example: At minute 4 a fictional sensor reports 25 degrees Celsius. The device sends the reading, the server receives it, and a rule flags values above 24. The flag is an output of analysis. It is not evidence that a real fan has switched on. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Compare two IoT careers: tasks, required skills, and what each contributes to this greenhouse. Use the reading as a source and identify one question for future research. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w2d5", "week": 2, "day": 5, "title": "Network design review", "standards": ["NI.N.8.a", "NI.N.8.b", "NI.N.8.c", "NI.IOT.8.b"], "reading": ["A network design must explain both normal behavior and failure behavior. A diagram should name devices, label connections, show where data is stored, and identify protocols by purpose. A review checks whether another person can trace a complete request without guessing what an unlabeled arrow means.", "Reliability is one design goal; privacy, accessibility, cost, and speed matter too. A greenhouse alert must be understandable to a person who cannot distinguish red from green. A text label such as high temperature gives information independently of color. A retry plan must avoid confusing an old reading with a fresh one.", "Use evidence from your packet trials and lifecycle model to revise your design. State which parts you actually tested and which remain predictions. The weekly test checks concepts; your design report shows whether you can apply those concepts to a system."], "example": "A design labels the link wireless but omits the access point. During review, the designer adds its function, identifies the router boundary, and labels where the server receives readings. The revised model lets a reviewer locate a possible connection failure.", "tasks": ["Create a complete virtual greenhouse network diagram. Include hardware roles, data direction, storage, and two protocol purposes.", "Use Network Lab to test a missing reading scenario. Explain recovery and a safe fallback.", "Review your design with a partner or use a second-role self-review. Record a revision and submit the Week 2 report and teacher-assigned test."], "exit": "What evidence supports your design's reliability claim?", "lab": "network", "starter": "", "extension": ["Trace an information request in both directions. The client sends a request toward a server, and the server sends a response back through network links. The response need not travel through exactly the same sequence of routers in a real network. Our diagram uses a single simplified path to make the roles visible. A connection between two points can work while another part fails, so a useful test identifies which portion it exercised.", "Addresses help systems deliver information to a destination. People usually type a readable domain name; a name-resolution service helps locate the corresponding network address. A domain name, an address, and a page's content are different things. A correct address does not prove that the content is reliable. Similarly, an encrypted connection protects communication on the path but does not decide whether the person operating the destination is honest.", "Reliable communication requires more than sending data once. A receiving system may need to recognize order, acknowledge receipt, detect missing information, and obtain it again. These activities consume time and capacity. A live application may make a different tradeoff from a file transfer because an old update can be less useful than a fresh one. Explain the application's goal before judging a network strategy.", "When drawing a network model, label each arrow with what moves along it and each node with its role. Include where information is gathered, received, stored, and analyzed. Add a failure condition and a safe response. A temperature alert design might preserve recent readings locally, label stale data clearly, and ask a person to check conditions when fresh readings stop. That plan connects the networking model to a human decision instead of treating successful delivery as the only goal.", "A useful network test has a defined starting point and destination. Write what you sent, where it was supposed to go, and what arrived. If a message is incomplete, distinguish a missing piece from a piece received in the wrong order. Both require interpretation rules, but the evidence differs. A diagram with numbered pieces and acknowledgments helps a reader follow the process without guessing. When a service resumes after a retry, report the successful recovery and the extra step required. That evidence supports a reliability claim within the model while leaving real-world timing and capacity questions open for future investigation."], "support": "Revisit this example: A design labels the link wireless but omits the access point. During review, the designer adds its function, identifies the router boundary, and labels where the server receives readings. The revised model lets a reviewer locate a possible connection failure. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Review your design with a partner or use a second-role self-review. Record a revision and submit the Week 2 report and teacher-assigned test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Cybersecurity and digital trust", "goal": "Investigate fictional threats, compare protection measures, and explore historical encryption.", "lessons": [{"id": "w3d1", "week": 3, "day": 1, "title": "Layers of protection", "standards": ["NI.C.8.a", "IC.SLE.8.c"], "reading": ["Information security protects confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Confidentiality limits who can read information. Integrity protects it from unauthorized change. Availability means authorized people can access it when needed. A design can improve one goal while creating a difficulty for another, such as a strong lock that also blocks an authorized user who loses access.", "Physical protections include locked rooms, controlled device storage, and supervision. Digital protections include access permissions, authentication, encryption, and updates. Encryption makes data unreadable without appropriate key material. Authentication checks a claimed identity. Neither makes the other unnecessary.", "Use fictional examples in our security lab. Never type real passwords or try to test a real account. If you encounter an actual warning or suspected incident, tell the teacher through the normal school process. This course models defensive decisions, not attacks on real systems."], "example": "A stolen laptop with encryption and a strong sign-in may keep stored records unreadable, but the owner still needs a backup to regain availability. A locked cabinet may reduce theft risk but does not protect data sent over an unprotected connection.", "tasks": ["Analyze Security Desk case 4. Identify confidentiality, integrity, and availability risks.", "Design two physical and three digital protections for fictional school records. State which risk each addresses.", "Explain a tradeoff and a safe recovery process for an authorized user who cannot sign in."], "exit": "Why do a locked room and encryption address different risks?", "lab": "security", "starter": "", "extension": ["Evaluate a security claim by asking what asset is at risk and what action the message requests. An unexpected request for a secret, installation, payment, or permission deserves verification through a channel you already know. Do not use the suspicious message's own contact details as the only source of verification. In a school setting, the trusted route is the teacher or the school's established support process, not an improvised investigation of a real sender.", "Different safeguards address different stages of risk. A locked storage area can reduce theft. Account permissions can limit which files a signed-in user may open. Encryption can reduce disclosure if protected data is intercepted or stored media is lost. A backup can help recover from damaged or inaccessible files. An update can correct a known software weakness. None of these measures eliminates the need for the others, and each depends on suitable configuration.", "Protection also includes how an organization treats people. Collect only information needed for a stated purpose, explain access, and avoid retaining data without a reason. A service can create risk through its ordinary collection practices even when nobody has installed malware. Think about the consequences if a record were exposed or altered. A fictional student project should use invented records because learning the concept does not require exposing classmates' information.", "Keep observations and attributions separate. You can observe a threatening request without knowing who wrote it. You can notice unusual behavior without knowing which program caused it. Write a report with the relevant facts, the action you avoided, and the trusted person you contacted. Historical cipher exercises demonstrate how representations change with rules and keys; they do not give permission to test protected systems or provide an adequate method for securing real confidential records.", "Consider the difference between suspicion and certainty when communicating a security concern. A report can be useful without identifying a specific attacker or malicious program. Include the time within the fictional scenario, the message or behavior observed, the requested action, and the action you took instead. Avoid forwarding suspicious links to classmates as a warning because doing so can spread the risky content. A safe classroom report describes the warning signs in plain text and gives the trusted reporting route. The goal is to protect people and preserve useful information, not to create excitement around the threat or make unsupported accusations."], "support": "Revisit this example: A stolen laptop with encryption and a strong sign-in may keep stored records unreadable, but the owner still needs a backup to regain availability. A locked cabinet may reduce theft risk but does not protect data sent over an unprotected connection. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Explain a tradeoff and a safe recovery process for an authorized user who cannot sign in. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w3d2", "week": 3, "day": 2, "title": "Malware is a hypothesis", "standards": ["NI.C.8.b", "CS.T.8.a"], "reading": ["Malware is software designed to cause harm or unauthorized activity. Ransomware blocks access or encrypts data to demand payment. Spyware collects information secretly. A worm can spread across systems; a virus attaches to other software and spreads when that software runs. Some malicious programs combine behaviors.", "Symptoms such as slowness, unwanted pop-ups, changed settings, or missing files can suggest a problem, but they do not prove a specific infection. Network failures and ordinary software bugs can create similar symptoms. Preserve evidence, stop suspicious activity, and report the problem. Do not download a tool offered by the suspicious message itself.", "Defensive strategies reduce opportunities for harm: approved updates, trusted installation sources, limited permissions, backups, and skeptical handling of unexpected requests. School-managed devices may restrict installations and security settings. Respect those restrictions and let the responsible adults investigate actual incidents."], "example": "A pop-up claims an infection and offers a cleaner. Installing it would trust the warning itself as a source. A safer action is to close or leave the suspicious content if permitted and report through a known channel. A warning in an untrusted page is not a trusted diagnosis.", "tasks": ["Use Security Desk case 3. Explain the evidence for your response.", "Compare ransomware, spyware, and worms by behavior, likely impact, and useful defensive layers.", "Write a report separating observations from hypotheses for a slow browser with unwanted pop-ups. Give a safe next step without claiming a confirmed infection."], "exit": "Why is a slow computer insufficient evidence to identify malware?", "lab": "security", "starter": "", "extension": ["Evaluate a security claim by asking what asset is at risk and what action the message requests. An unexpected request for a secret, installation, payment, or permission deserves verification through a channel you already know. Do not use the suspicious message's own contact details as the only source of verification. In a school setting, the trusted route is the teacher or the school's established support process, not an improvised investigation of a real sender.", "Different safeguards address different stages of risk. A locked storage area can reduce theft. Account permissions can limit which files a signed-in user may open. Encryption can reduce disclosure if protected data is intercepted or stored media is lost. A backup can help recover from damaged or inaccessible files. An update can correct a known software weakness. None of these measures eliminates the need for the others, and each depends on suitable configuration.", "Protection also includes how an organization treats people. Collect only information needed for a stated purpose, explain access, and avoid retaining data without a reason. A service can create risk through its ordinary collection practices even when nobody has installed malware. Think about the consequences if a record were exposed or altered. A fictional student project should use invented records because learning the concept does not require exposing classmates' information.", "Keep observations and attributions separate. You can observe a threatening request without knowing who wrote it. You can notice unusual behavior without knowing which program caused it. Write a report with the relevant facts, the action you avoided, and the trusted person you contacted. Historical cipher exercises demonstrate how representations change with rules and keys; they do not give permission to test protected systems or provide an adequate method for securing real confidential records.", "Consider the difference between suspicion and certainty when communicating a security concern. A report can be useful without identifying a specific attacker or malicious program. Include the time within the fictional scenario, the message or behavior observed, the requested action, and the action you took instead. Avoid forwarding suspicious links to classmates as a warning because doing so can spread the risky content. A safe classroom report describes the warning signs in plain text and gives the trusted reporting route. The goal is to protect people and preserve useful information, not to create excitement around the threat or make unsupported accusations."], "support": "Revisit this example: A pop-up claims an infection and offers a cleaner. Installing it would trust the warning itself as a source. A safer action is to close or leave the suspicious content if permitted and report through a known channel. A warning in an untrusted page is not a trusted diagnosis. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write a report separating observations from hypotheses for a slow browser with unwanted pop-ups. Give a safe next step without claiming a confirmed infection. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w3d3", "week": 3, "day": 3, "title": "Who wants what?", "standards": ["NI.C.8.c", "IC.SLE.8.a", "IC.SLE.8.b"], "reading": ["A threat actor is a person or group that could harm a system or misuse information. Organized crime may seek money. A nation-state operation may seek strategic information or disruption. Hacktivists may act to promote a political or social cause. Cyberterrorist groups may seek fear or serious disruption. These are broad categories, not evidence that a particular group caused an incident.", "A threat vector is a route used to reach a target, such as a deceptive message, a malicious download, or a stolen account. Motive and method are different. Multiple kinds of actors can use the same method, so an email's appearance alone does not establish who sent it.", "Automated data collection can make targeting easier. Location history, browsing records, and metadata may reveal details a user did not deliberately post. Collecting less data, limiting access, and explaining data use reduce risk. A convenient service can still create privacy costs for individuals and organizations."], "example": "A fictional message asks for payment to restore access. Financial gain is a plausible motive, but the text alone does not prove the actor's identity. The vector could be a deceptive message. A report should state what is observed and avoid unsupported attribution.", "tasks": ["Analyze Security Desk case 1. Identify the request, pressure tactic, and trusted verification route.", "Compare four actor categories using motive, possible target, and a defensive measure. Do not name real suspects.", "Explain how automatically collected location data could create a privacy and economic risk. Recommend a data-minimization change."], "exit": "How does an actor's motive differ from a threat vector?", "lab": "security", "starter": "", "extension": ["Evaluate a security claim by asking what asset is at risk and what action the message requests. An unexpected request for a secret, installation, payment, or permission deserves verification through a channel you already know. Do not use the suspicious message's own contact details as the only source of verification. In a school setting, the trusted route is the teacher or the school's established support process, not an improvised investigation of a real sender.", "Different safeguards address different stages of risk. A locked storage area can reduce theft. Account permissions can limit which files a signed-in user may open. Encryption can reduce disclosure if protected data is intercepted or stored media is lost. A backup can help recover from damaged or inaccessible files. An update can correct a known software weakness. None of these measures eliminates the need for the others, and each depends on suitable configuration.", "Protection also includes how an organization treats people. Collect only information needed for a stated purpose, explain access, and avoid retaining data without a reason. A service can create risk through its ordinary collection practices even when nobody has installed malware. Think about the consequences if a record were exposed or altered. A fictional student project should use invented records because learning the concept does not require exposing classmates' information.", "Keep observations and attributions separate. You can observe a threatening request without knowing who wrote it. You can notice unusual behavior without knowing which program caused it. Write a report with the relevant facts, the action you avoided, and the trusted person you contacted. Historical cipher exercises demonstrate how representations change with rules and keys; they do not give permission to test protected systems or provide an adequate method for securing real confidential records.", "Consider the difference between suspicion and certainty when communicating a security concern. A report can be useful without identifying a specific attacker or malicious program. Include the time within the fictional scenario, the message or behavior observed, the requested action, and the action you took instead. Avoid forwarding suspicious links to classmates as a warning because doing so can spread the risky content. A safe classroom report describes the warning signs in plain text and gives the trusted reporting route. The goal is to protect people and preserve useful information, not to create excitement around the threat or make unsupported accusations."], "support": "Revisit this example: A fictional message asks for payment to restore access. Financial gain is a plausible motive, but the text alone does not prove the actor's identity. The vector could be a deceptive message. A report should state what is observed and avoid unsupported attribution. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Explain how automatically collected location data could create a privacy and economic risk. Recommend a data-minimization change. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w3d4", "week": 3, "day": 4, "title": "Ciphers and modern protection", "standards": ["NI.C.8.d"], "reading": ["Encryption transforms readable plaintext into ciphertext using a method and key. Decryption reverses the transformation with appropriate key information. Historical ciphers help reveal the idea but are not safe choices for modern confidential data.", "Vigenere shifts letters using a repeating keyword. With A as zero, add the plaintext letter number to the key letter number and wrap after Z. Bacon's cipher assigns letters to patterns of two symbols, often shown as A and B. Enigma used changing rotor substitutions, so its transformation changed as letters were processed. These methods differ in representation and how substitutions vary.", "Modern protection uses professionally designed and reviewed systems. Do not invent an encryption scheme for real secrets based on a classroom activity. Encryption can protect data in transit or at rest, but it does not stop every risk. A person who voluntarily sends a password to an attacker can bypass an otherwise secure connection."], "example": "For Vigenere plaintext HI and key AB, H=7 plus A=0 produces H; I=8 plus B=1 produces J. Ciphertext is HJ. By comparison, a five-symbol Bacon-style code represents a letter as a pattern rather than a numeric shift. Enigma changes substitutions through rotor movement.", "tasks": ["Use Security Lab to encrypt HELLO with KEY. Then change one key letter and compare the result.", "Manually encrypt CAT with key AB using A=0. Explain repeated-key alignment.", "Compare Vigenere, Bacon, and Enigma by representation and changing substitutions. State why none should be chosen to secure school records today."], "exit": "Why is knowing the encryption method different from knowing its key?", "lab": "security", "starter": "", "extension": ["Evaluate a security claim by asking what asset is at risk and what action the message requests. An unexpected request for a secret, installation, payment, or permission deserves verification through a channel you already know. Do not use the suspicious message's own contact details as the only source of verification. In a school setting, the trusted route is the teacher or the school's established support process, not an improvised investigation of a real sender.", "Different safeguards address different stages of risk. A locked storage area can reduce theft. Account permissions can limit which files a signed-in user may open. Encryption can reduce disclosure if protected data is intercepted or stored media is lost. A backup can help recover from damaged or inaccessible files. An update can correct a known software weakness. None of these measures eliminates the need for the others, and each depends on suitable configuration.", "Protection also includes how an organization treats people. Collect only information needed for a stated purpose, explain access, and avoid retaining data without a reason. A service can create risk through its ordinary collection practices even when nobody has installed malware. Think about the consequences if a record were exposed or altered. A fictional student project should use invented records because learning the concept does not require exposing classmates' information.", "Keep observations and attributions separate. You can observe a threatening request without knowing who wrote it. You can notice unusual behavior without knowing which program caused it. Write a report with the relevant facts, the action you avoided, and the trusted person you contacted. Historical cipher exercises demonstrate how representations change with rules and keys; they do not give permission to test protected systems or provide an adequate method for securing real confidential records.", "Consider the difference between suspicion and certainty when communicating a security concern. A report can be useful without identifying a specific attacker or malicious program. Include the time within the fictional scenario, the message or behavior observed, the requested action, and the action you took instead. Avoid forwarding suspicious links to classmates as a warning because doing so can spread the risky content. A safe classroom report describes the warning signs in plain text and gives the trusted reporting route. The goal is to protect people and preserve useful information, not to create excitement around the threat or make unsupported accusations."], "support": "Revisit this example: For Vigenere plaintext HI and key AB, H=7 plus A=0 produces H; I=8 plus B=1 produces J. Ciphertext is HJ. By comparison, a five-symbol Bacon-style code represents a letter as a pattern rather than a numeric shift. Enigma changes substitutions through rotor movement. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Compare Vigenere, Bacon, and Enigma by representation and changing substitutions. State why none should be chosen to secure school records today. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w3d5", "week": 3, "day": 5, "title": "Defend a fictional school service", "standards": ["NI.C.8.a", "NI.C.8.b", "NI.C.8.c", "NI.C.8.d", "IC.SLE.8.c"], "reading": ["A security plan connects assets, threats, protections, and recovery. An asset is something valuable, such as student work, confidential records, or access to lessons. A threat is a possible source of harm. A protection should address a specific risk rather than simply sound technical.", "Consider prevention, detection, and response. Permissions and updates can reduce risk. A reporting channel helps people detect and communicate a suspicious event. Backups and a recovery plan help restore access. Training matters because a person can be deceived into sharing information even when the network uses encryption.", "A useful plan avoids promises of perfect safety. Explain what your measures protect, what risks remain, and who handles actual incidents. Privacy responsibilities include collecting only needed information, restricting access, and explaining retention. Your school decides its actual policies; your task is to reason about a fictional system."], "example": "A fictional learning service stores drafts. Plan: restrict access to each student's work, encrypt transfers, keep recoverable backups, and provide a trusted reporting route. Remaining risk: a user might share a file publicly. A clear sharing policy and review reduce that risk.", "tasks": ["Complete two Security Desk scenarios and include the reasoning for each response.", "Write a one-page protection plan for a fictional learning service. Include assets, three threats, five protections, recovery, and remaining risk.", "Compare one historical cipher with modern protected communication at a conceptual level. Submit your plan and complete the Week 3 test."], "exit": "Which measure in your plan protects availability?", "lab": "security", "starter": "", "extension": ["Evaluate a security claim by asking what asset is at risk and what action the message requests. An unexpected request for a secret, installation, payment, or permission deserves verification through a channel you already know. Do not use the suspicious message's own contact details as the only source of verification. In a school setting, the trusted route is the teacher or the school's established support process, not an improvised investigation of a real sender.", "Different safeguards address different stages of risk. A locked storage area can reduce theft. Account permissions can limit which files a signed-in user may open. Encryption can reduce disclosure if protected data is intercepted or stored media is lost. A backup can help recover from damaged or inaccessible files. An update can correct a known software weakness. None of these measures eliminates the need for the others, and each depends on suitable configuration.", "Protection also includes how an organization treats people. Collect only information needed for a stated purpose, explain access, and avoid retaining data without a reason. A service can create risk through its ordinary collection practices even when nobody has installed malware. Think about the consequences if a record were exposed or altered. A fictional student project should use invented records because learning the concept does not require exposing classmates' information.", "Keep observations and attributions separate. You can observe a threatening request without knowing who wrote it. You can notice unusual behavior without knowing which program caused it. Write a report with the relevant facts, the action you avoided, and the trusted person you contacted. Historical cipher exercises demonstrate how representations change with rules and keys; they do not give permission to test protected systems or provide an adequate method for securing real confidential records.", "Consider the difference between suspicion and certainty when communicating a security concern. A report can be useful without identifying a specific attacker or malicious program. Include the time within the fictional scenario, the message or behavior observed, the requested action, and the action you took instead. Avoid forwarding suspicious links to classmates as a warning because doing so can spread the risky content. A safe classroom report describes the warning signs in plain text and gives the trusted reporting route. The goal is to protect people and preserve useful information, not to create excitement around the threat or make unsupported accusations."], "support": "Revisit this example: A fictional learning service stores drafts. Plan: restrict access to each student's work, encrypt transfers, keep recoverable backups, and provide a trusted reporting route. Remaining risk: a user might share a file publicly. A clear sharing policy and review reduce that risk. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Compare one historical cipher with modern protected communication at a conceptual level. Submit your plan and complete the Week 3 test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Data that tells a trustworthy story", "goal": "Organize fictional data, build spreadsheet formulas and graphs, and test models.", "lessons": [{"id": "w4d1", "week": 4, "day": 1, "title": "Collect only what you need", "standards": ["DA.DCS.8.a", "IC.SLE.8.a"], "reading": ["Data collection starts with a question. To investigate greenhouse temperature over time, collect timestamps and temperatures with units. Student names and home addresses are unnecessary. A useful data dictionary defines each field, its type, units, and allowed values.", "Digital collection tools include sensors, forms, and observation logs. A sensor may record often but be miscalibrated. A form may capture opinions but reach only people who choose to respond. Missing values, inconsistent units, and duplicate entries can distort analysis. A blank reading is not automatically zero.", "Use fictional data throughout this course. Explain where data came from and what it can support. Good collection is not simply gathering the largest possible table. It means collecting relevant, interpretable information while respecting privacy and documenting limitations."], "example": "Question: did temperature rise during five minutes? Fields: minute as a number and temperature in degrees Celsius. A blank temperature means missing, not freezing. Mixing Fahrenheit and Celsius without conversion makes the comparison misleading.", "tasks": ["Inspect Data Lab's five rows and write a data dictionary.", "Create a fictional eight-row collection with one missing value and one inconsistent unit. Explain how you would resolve each problem without inventing a measurement.", "Compare a sensor and a survey as tools for your question. Identify unnecessary personal data and remove it from your proposed design."], "exit": "Why should a missing measurement not be silently replaced with zero?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["A trustworthy analysis begins before a graph is drawn. State the question, define fields and units, and decide how to handle missing or invalid entries. Preserve the original table so a reviewer can distinguish measured values from later changes. If a value seems unusual, investigate its source and context. Removing it merely to make a smoother graph changes the evidence without justification.", "A spreadsheet makes relationships explicit. A cell can contain an input value, a formula, or a label. A formula that refers to the correct range can update when inputs change, but it still needs checking. Test a small example you can calculate manually. When copying a formula, inspect how relative references move. A formula that skips the last row or includes the wrong column can produce a plausible-looking result that answers a different question.", "Representations serve readers with different needs. A graph should be accompanied by a clear written description and accessible source values. Use labels and units so the reader does not have to infer meaning from color or position alone. When values vary over time, preserve their order. When comparing categories, use a representation that makes the category distinction clear. Always identify the data source and the period or sample represented.", "A claim must fit the collection method and size. Five fictional readings support practice with a model; they do not establish a real trend for an entire school or season. Distinguish a description of what happened in the table from a prediction about future data and from an explanation of cause. Include at least one limitation and one next measurement that would strengthen the investigation. This makes the analysis useful even when the original data set is small.", "Check that the storage format fits the information you intend to preserve. A CSV stores rows and columns of text values separated by delimiters; it does not preserve a spreadsheet graph, cell formatting, or a workbook containing several sheets. A spreadsheet file can retain those additional features. If you export results, keep the original working artifact as well as the submitted representation. A screenshot can show a graph but may not reveal its formulas. Include enough evidence for a reviewer to reconstruct the calculation. This is why the assignment asks for both visible results and the relationships that produced them."], "support": "Revisit this example: Question: did temperature rise during five minutes? Fields: minute as a number and temperature in degrees Celsius. A blank temperature means missing, not freezing. Mixing Fahrenheit and Celsius without conversion makes the comparison misleading. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Compare a sensor and a survey as tools for your question. Identify unnecessary personal data and remove it from your proposed design. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w4d2", "week": 4, "day": 2, "title": "Storage and a file structure", "standards": ["DA.DCS.8.b", "DA.DCS.8.c"], "reading": ["Local storage keeps files on a device. Cloud storage keeps them on remote systems accessed through a network. Both rely on physical storage hardware. A local file may be available without a connection but can be lost if the device fails. A cloud file may support collaboration, but access depends on permissions and sometimes connectivity.", "A logical file structure groups related work and uses consistent names. A folder called Week04/Data makes a file easier to find than a collection of unnamed downloads. Names such as greenhouse_raw_v1.csv and greenhouse_clean_v2.csv communicate purpose and version. Preserve an original data file before cleaning it.", "Collaboration also needs rules about who can view, edit, and submit. Shared access is not the same as public access. Use the minimum sharing needed for your task. Our course site stores drafts locally when the browser permits it, but it does not back them up to a server or automatically submit them."], "example": "Structure: CS8 → Week04 → raw, analysis, and final. Keep the original sensor table in raw; put formulas and graphs in analysis; put the submitted report in final. A partner can edit the analysis copy while the original remains unchanged.", "tasks": ["Design a folder tree for a nine-week course, including original data, working files, and final submissions.", "Compare where a local download and a cloud document live, how they are accessed, and one risk for each.", "Export a lesson response, then restore it using the site. Record what restored and explain why the file still needs Classroom submission."], "exit": "Why preserve an original data file before cleaning?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["A trustworthy analysis begins before a graph is drawn. State the question, define fields and units, and decide how to handle missing or invalid entries. Preserve the original table so a reviewer can distinguish measured values from later changes. If a value seems unusual, investigate its source and context. Removing it merely to make a smoother graph changes the evidence without justification.", "A spreadsheet makes relationships explicit. A cell can contain an input value, a formula, or a label. A formula that refers to the correct range can update when inputs change, but it still needs checking. Test a small example you can calculate manually. When copying a formula, inspect how relative references move. A formula that skips the last row or includes the wrong column can produce a plausible-looking result that answers a different question.", "Representations serve readers with different needs. A graph should be accompanied by a clear written description and accessible source values. Use labels and units so the reader does not have to infer meaning from color or position alone. When values vary over time, preserve their order. When comparing categories, use a representation that makes the category distinction clear. Always identify the data source and the period or sample represented.", "A claim must fit the collection method and size. Five fictional readings support practice with a model; they do not establish a real trend for an entire school or season. Distinguish a description of what happened in the table from a prediction about future data and from an explanation of cause. Include at least one limitation and one next measurement that would strengthen the investigation. This makes the analysis useful even when the original data set is small.", "Check that the storage format fits the information you intend to preserve. A CSV stores rows and columns of text values separated by delimiters; it does not preserve a spreadsheet graph, cell formatting, or a workbook containing several sheets. A spreadsheet file can retain those additional features. If you export results, keep the original working artifact as well as the submitted representation. A screenshot can show a graph but may not reveal its formulas. Include enough evidence for a reviewer to reconstruct the calculation. This is why the assignment asks for both visible results and the relationships that produced them."], "support": "Revisit this example: Structure: CS8 → Week04 → raw, analysis, and final. Keep the original sensor table in raw; put formulas and graphs in analysis; put the submitted report in final. A partner can edit the analysis copy while the original remains unchanged. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Export a lesson response, then restore it using the site. Record what restored and explain why the file still needs Classroom submission. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w4d3", "week": 4, "day": 3, "title": "Spreadsheet formulas explain changes", "standards": ["DA.VC.8.b"], "reading": ["A spreadsheet organizes values in cells. Column letters and row numbers identify a cell, such as B2. A formula begins with an equals sign and computes a result. A function is a named operation used inside a formula. SUM adds values; AVERAGE calculates a mean. A range such as B2:B6 includes both endpoints.", "Relative references change when a formula is copied. If C3 contains =B3-B2, copying it down one row changes the references to =B4-B3. This computes each consecutive change. A hard-coded answer such as 2 will not update when the source data changes. Formulas make the relationship visible and reusable.", "Check outputs with a small manual calculation. Formatting a cell does not repair a wrong formula. A result can look polished and still use the wrong range. Select a graph that matches the question and label quantities and units. Time-ordered readings often suit a line graph; category totals often suit a bar graph."], "example": "For temperatures 18,20,19,23,25 the sum is 105 and mean is 21. The change from the first to second reading is 20-18=2. A spreadsheet with headers in row 1 uses =AVERAGE(B2:B6) and =B3-B2. Check that the header is not included as a measurement.", "tasks": ["Copy Data Lab's default CSV into a new Google Sheet. If Sheets is unavailable, use the written formula model and Data Lab calculations, then complete Sheets when access returns.", "Create SUM and AVERAGE formulas and a consecutive-change column. Copy the relative formula down. Change one reading and record updated outputs.", "Create a labeled line graph. Explain why it fits the question. Submit the Sheet or an accessible screenshot plus formulas and explanation."], "exit": "Why does a formula update when a source value changes?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["A trustworthy analysis begins before a graph is drawn. State the question, define fields and units, and decide how to handle missing or invalid entries. Preserve the original table so a reviewer can distinguish measured values from later changes. If a value seems unusual, investigate its source and context. Removing it merely to make a smoother graph changes the evidence without justification.", "A spreadsheet makes relationships explicit. A cell can contain an input value, a formula, or a label. A formula that refers to the correct range can update when inputs change, but it still needs checking. Test a small example you can calculate manually. When copying a formula, inspect how relative references move. A formula that skips the last row or includes the wrong column can produce a plausible-looking result that answers a different question.", "Representations serve readers with different needs. A graph should be accompanied by a clear written description and accessible source values. Use labels and units so the reader does not have to infer meaning from color or position alone. When values vary over time, preserve their order. When comparing categories, use a representation that makes the category distinction clear. Always identify the data source and the period or sample represented.", "A claim must fit the collection method and size. Five fictional readings support practice with a model; they do not establish a real trend for an entire school or season. Distinguish a description of what happened in the table from a prediction about future data and from an explanation of cause. Include at least one limitation and one next measurement that would strengthen the investigation. This makes the analysis useful even when the original data set is small.", "Check that the storage format fits the information you intend to preserve. A CSV stores rows and columns of text values separated by delimiters; it does not preserve a spreadsheet graph, cell formatting, or a workbook containing several sheets. A spreadsheet file can retain those additional features. If you export results, keep the original working artifact as well as the submitted representation. A screenshot can show a graph but may not reveal its formulas. Include enough evidence for a reviewer to reconstruct the calculation. This is why the assignment asks for both visible results and the relationships that produced them."], "support": "Revisit this example: For temperatures 18,20,19,23,25 the sum is 105 and mean is 21. The change from the first to second reading is 20-18=2. A spreadsheet with headers in row 1 uses =AVERAGE(B2:B6) and =B3-B2. Check that the header is not included as a measurement. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Create a labeled line graph. Explain why it fits the question. Submit the Sheet or an accessible screenshot plus formulas and explanation. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w4d4", "week": 4, "day": 4, "title": "Graphs can hide as well as reveal", "standards": ["DA.VC.8.a", "DA.VC.8.b"], "reading": ["A representation selects what to show. A graph can help a reader see change, compare categories, or notice unusual values. It can also mislead through missing labels, a distorted scale, or a selective time period. Ask what question the graph answers and what data it leaves out.", "A mean summarizes a collection but can hide variation. Two sets can share a mean while having very different spreads. A single unusual reading may change a mean. Investigate whether it is a genuine event or a recording problem; do not remove it only because it weakens your preferred claim.", "Good communication includes a title, labeled axes, units, a source description, and a claim proportionate to the evidence. A temperature rise during five minutes does not establish a long-term climate trend or prove what caused the change. Distinguish description from causal explanation."], "example": "Set A: 20,21,22. Set B: 10,21,32. Both have mean 21, but B varies much more. A graph showing only the mean hides this difference. A line graph of all readings makes the difference visible.", "tasks": ["Use Data Lab to analyze both sets from the example. Record the mean, minimum, and maximum.", "In your Sheet create a comparison graph with clear labels. Describe how a narrow vertical scale changes the visual impression.", "Write a claim, two supporting observations, and a limitation. Revise a misleading graph description so it matches the data."], "exit": "Why is a matching mean insufficient to show that two data sets behave alike?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["A trustworthy analysis begins before a graph is drawn. State the question, define fields and units, and decide how to handle missing or invalid entries. Preserve the original table so a reviewer can distinguish measured values from later changes. If a value seems unusual, investigate its source and context. Removing it merely to make a smoother graph changes the evidence without justification.", "A spreadsheet makes relationships explicit. A cell can contain an input value, a formula, or a label. A formula that refers to the correct range can update when inputs change, but it still needs checking. Test a small example you can calculate manually. When copying a formula, inspect how relative references move. A formula that skips the last row or includes the wrong column can produce a plausible-looking result that answers a different question.", "Representations serve readers with different needs. A graph should be accompanied by a clear written description and accessible source values. Use labels and units so the reader does not have to infer meaning from color or position alone. When values vary over time, preserve their order. When comparing categories, use a representation that makes the category distinction clear. Always identify the data source and the period or sample represented.", "A claim must fit the collection method and size. Five fictional readings support practice with a model; they do not establish a real trend for an entire school or season. Distinguish a description of what happened in the table from a prediction about future data and from an explanation of cause. Include at least one limitation and one next measurement that would strengthen the investigation. This makes the analysis useful even when the original data set is small.", "Check that the storage format fits the information you intend to preserve. A CSV stores rows and columns of text values separated by delimiters; it does not preserve a spreadsheet graph, cell formatting, or a workbook containing several sheets. A spreadsheet file can retain those additional features. If you export results, keep the original working artifact as well as the submitted representation. A screenshot can show a graph but may not reveal its formulas. Include enough evidence for a reviewer to reconstruct the calculation. This is why the assignment asks for both visible results and the relationships that produced them."], "support": "Revisit this example: Set A: 20,21,22. Set B: 10,21,32. Both have mean 21, but B varies much more. A graph showing only the mean hides this difference. A line graph of all readings makes the difference visible. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write a claim, two supporting observations, and a limitation. Revise a misleading graph description so it matches the data. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w4d5", "week": 4, "day": 5, "title": "Test a model before trusting it", "standards": ["DA.IM.8.a", "DA.VC.8.a", "DA.VC.8.b"], "reading": ["A model represents selected features of a system. A threshold model might predict an alert whenever temperature exceeds 24 degrees Celsius. It is simpler than a real greenhouse, which has changing sunlight, humidity, sensor error, and equipment delays. A model is useful when its assumptions fit the question.", "A hypothesis predicts what will happen under stated conditions. Test ordinary values, boundary values, and unusual inputs. A strict greater-than rule excludes the boundary itself. Missing data requires a separate policy; treating it as a normal low value could hide a dangerous lack of information.", "Compare predictions with observations, revise the model, and describe what the revised model can support. A model that matches five examples is not proven correct for every future situation. Your report should show both the useful result and its limits."], "example": "Rule: alert if temperature >24. Test 23 gives no alert, 24 gives no alert, and 25 gives alert. If the requirement says at least 24, the rule should use >=24 instead. The boundary test reveals the mismatch.", "tasks": ["Create a threshold model in your Google Sheet with =IF(B2>24,\"alert\",\"normal\"). Copy down and test 23,24,25.", "Use Data Lab to examine an unusual reading. Explain whether your evidence supports keeping or questioning it.", "Submit a data report with collection method, file structure, formulas, graph, three test cases, revision, and model limitation. Complete the Week 4 test."], "exit": "Which boundary test would distinguish >24 from >=24?", "lab": "data", "starter": "", "extension": ["A trustworthy analysis begins before a graph is drawn. State the question, define fields and units, and decide how to handle missing or invalid entries. Preserve the original table so a reviewer can distinguish measured values from later changes. If a value seems unusual, investigate its source and context. Removing it merely to make a smoother graph changes the evidence without justification.", "A spreadsheet makes relationships explicit. A cell can contain an input value, a formula, or a label. A formula that refers to the correct range can update when inputs change, but it still needs checking. Test a small example you can calculate manually. When copying a formula, inspect how relative references move. A formula that skips the last row or includes the wrong column can produce a plausible-looking result that answers a different question.", "Representations serve readers with different needs. A graph should be accompanied by a clear written description and accessible source values. Use labels and units so the reader does not have to infer meaning from color or position alone. When values vary over time, preserve their order. When comparing categories, use a representation that makes the category distinction clear. Always identify the data source and the period or sample represented.", "A claim must fit the collection method and size. Five fictional readings support practice with a model; they do not establish a real trend for an entire school or season. Distinguish a description of what happened in the table from a prediction about future data and from an explanation of cause. Include at least one limitation and one next measurement that would strengthen the investigation. This makes the analysis useful even when the original data set is small.", "Check that the storage format fits the information you intend to preserve. A CSV stores rows and columns of text values separated by delimiters; it does not preserve a spreadsheet graph, cell formatting, or a workbook containing several sheets. A spreadsheet file can retain those additional features. If you export results, keep the original working artifact as well as the submitted representation. A screenshot can show a graph but may not reveal its formulas. Include enough evidence for a reviewer to reconstruct the calculation. This is why the assignment asks for both visible results and the relationships that produced them."], "support": "Revisit this example: Rule: alert if temperature >24. Test 23 gives no alert, 24 gives no alert, and 25 gives alert. If the requirement says at least 24, the rule should use >=24 instead. The boundary test reveals the mismatch. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Submit a data report with collection method, file structure, formulas, graph, three test cases, revision, and model limitation. Complete the Week 4 test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Code with evidence", "goal": "Write JavaScript using variables, collections, decisions, loops, and parameterized procedures.", "lessons": [{"id": "w5d1", "week": 5, "day": 1, "title": "Two algorithms one task", "standards": ["ATP.A.8.a", "ATP.M.8.a"], "reading": ["An algorithm is a finite sequence of steps for a task. Pseudocode expresses those steps in readable language without requiring exact programming syntax. A task can have several correct algorithms. Compare them using a stated measure such as number of checks, clarity, or memory use.", "Decomposition divides a problem into manageable parts. To count high temperatures, separate reading the collection, comparing each value, counting matches, and reporting the answer. Small parts are easier to test. A procedure names a reusable group of steps.", "Efficiency depends on the problem. A solution that sorts a list before counting high values may do unnecessary work. Another solution scans once and counts directly. Both can be correct, but one pass is a better fit when the only requirement is a count. Explain your comparison rather than claiming that shorter code is always faster."], "example": "Algorithm A: for each reading, if above 20 add one to count. Algorithm B: sort readings, then locate the first above 20 and count the remaining readings. For one count from unsorted data, A avoids sorting. On [18,23,25], both report 2.", "tasks": ["Write two pseudocode solutions to count values above a threshold. Identify their subproblems.", "Trace each solution on [18,23,25] and [20,20]. Count comparisons or explain another consistent efficiency measure.", "Run the starter in Coding Lab. Match each code section to your pseudocode and justify a preferred solution."], "exit": "Why is character count a weak measure of algorithm efficiency?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["Read a program as a sequence of state changes. Before a loop begins, identify the inputs and initial variable values. At each iteration, record the current element, the condition's result, and any updated accumulator. After the loop, compare the returned value with the requirement. This manual trace can expose a problem even before the code is run, especially when a return statement appears in the wrong place.", "Collections and procedures let one solution apply to many examples. An array groups related values in order; a parameter lets a function receive a different collection or limit. Avoid copying an entire function just to change one threshold. Instead, choose a parameter name that explains its role and test several calls. A clear procedure contract states accepted inputs, output meaning, and behavior for unusual cases such as an empty collection.", "Testing is an investigation rather than a ceremony. Select cases that could reveal different failures. An empty collection tests initialization and the no-iteration path. A boundary value tests the exact comparison. A negative number tests an assumption that all values are positive. An invalid type tests whether the input contract is enforced. Write expected results independently, then run and compare. If a case fails, use the trace to locate the first point where behavior differs from the plan.", "JavaScript syntax and program logic are separate concerns. A missing brace can stop code from running, while an incorrect comparison can run successfully and still answer the wrong question. Fix the actual defect, keep a record of the evidence, and rerun earlier tests. Our coding window is temporary; preserve code in your response or assigned document. Use the output to support a claim about tested behavior, not as proof that every possible input will work.", "Use output deliberately while debugging. Print an intermediate value to check a hypothesis about the program, then compare it with the manual trace. A stream of unexplained output can make a defect harder to locate. Label the input and result so another reader knows which test produced each line. If the program fails to run, read the reported location and inspect nearby syntax before changing unrelated logic. If it runs with an incorrect answer, check the requirement, conditions, loop updates, and return position. The distinction helps you make a small justified repair instead of rewriting code without understanding the cause."], "support": "Revisit this example: Algorithm A: for each reading, if above 20 add one to count. Algorithm B: sort readings, then locate the first above 20 and count the remaining readings. For one count from unsorted data, A avoids sorting. On [18,23,25], both report 2. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Run the starter in Coding Lab. Match each code section to your pseudocode and justify a preferred solution. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w5d2", "week": 5, "day": 2, "title": "Variables and collections", "standards": ["ATP.VDR.8.a", "ATP.VDR.8.b", "ATP.PD.8.a"], "reading": ["A variable gives a name to a value. In JavaScript, const prevents reassignment of that variable binding; let allows reassignment. A number such as 20 differs from the text \"20\". Choosing suitable types makes a program's meaning clearer.", "An array is an ordered collection. JavaScript arrays use zero-based indexes: the first element has index 0. A collection lets one algorithm handle many values without a separate variable for every item. A loop can visit each value in order.", "A valid input range should be stated. A temperature-count function might accept a finite collection of numbers and a numeric threshold. Empty collections, negative values, exact-boundary values, and invalid text deserve explicit consideration. Decide what the program should do before using a convenient output as proof that it is correct."], "example": "const readings=[18,20,25]; readings[0] is 18 and readings.length is 3. A count starts at 0 and increases for matching elements. For an empty array the count should remain 0. It should not invent a reading.", "tasks": ["Create an array of six fictional readings and print the first element and length.", "Modify the starter to count values above 22. Predict results for empty, one-element, and mixed arrays before running.", "State the accepted input types and range. Explain how you would reject invalid data instead of silently treating it as valid."], "exit": "Why does the first element use index 0 rather than index 1?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["Read a program as a sequence of state changes. Before a loop begins, identify the inputs and initial variable values. At each iteration, record the current element, the condition's result, and any updated accumulator. After the loop, compare the returned value with the requirement. This manual trace can expose a problem even before the code is run, especially when a return statement appears in the wrong place.", "Collections and procedures let one solution apply to many examples. An array groups related values in order; a parameter lets a function receive a different collection or limit. Avoid copying an entire function just to change one threshold. Instead, choose a parameter name that explains its role and test several calls. A clear procedure contract states accepted inputs, output meaning, and behavior for unusual cases such as an empty collection.", "Testing is an investigation rather than a ceremony. Select cases that could reveal different failures. An empty collection tests initialization and the no-iteration path. A boundary value tests the exact comparison. A negative number tests an assumption that all values are positive. An invalid type tests whether the input contract is enforced. Write expected results independently, then run and compare. If a case fails, use the trace to locate the first point where behavior differs from the plan.", "JavaScript syntax and program logic are separate concerns. A missing brace can stop code from running, while an incorrect comparison can run successfully and still answer the wrong question. Fix the actual defect, keep a record of the evidence, and rerun earlier tests. Our coding window is temporary; preserve code in your response or assigned document. Use the output to support a claim about tested behavior, not as proof that every possible input will work.", "Use output deliberately while debugging. Print an intermediate value to check a hypothesis about the program, then compare it with the manual trace. A stream of unexplained output can make a defect harder to locate. Label the input and result so another reader knows which test produced each line. If the program fails to run, read the reported location and inspect nearby syntax before changing unrelated logic. If it runs with an incorrect answer, check the requirement, conditions, loop updates, and return position. The distinction helps you make a small justified repair instead of rewriting code without understanding the cause."], "support": "Revisit this example: const readings=[18,20,25]; readings[0] is 18 and readings.length is 3. A count starts at 0 and increases for matching elements. For an empty array the count should remain 0. It should not invent a reading. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: State the accepted input types and range. Explain how you would reject invalid data instead of silently treating it as valid. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w5d3", "week": 5, "day": 3, "title": "Decisions and boundaries", "standards": ["ATP.CS.8.a", "ATP.VDR.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.b"], "reading": ["A conditional selects behavior based on a Boolean value, true or false. The comparison x>20 differs from x>=20 at exactly 20. A boundary is where behavior changes. Testing just ordinary values may miss the error that occurs at that boundary.", "Use if and else to express alternatives clearly. In JavaScript === checks equality without converting types. The assignment operator = stores a value; it is not an equality test. A logical error produces an incorrect result even when the program runs without a crash.", "Translate words into exact conditions. More than means >. At least means >=. Between may be ambiguous unless the endpoints are specified. Clarify the requirement before writing code. Test below, at, and above each cutoff, then compare actual output with the expected behavior."], "example": "Requirement: alert at 24 or higher. Code if(t>24) misses 24. Tests 23 and 25 pass, but test 24 fails. Replacing > with >= fixes the boundary, and rerunning all three tests checks the revision.", "tasks": ["Write a function alert(t) that returns \"alert\" at 24 or above and \"normal\" otherwise.", "Predict and run tests 23,24,25. Introduce a > bug, record the failure, then repair it.", "Add an invalid input policy and explain the valid solution range. Distinguish a logical error from a program that cannot execute."], "exit": "Which input exposes an incorrect strict comparison for an at-least requirement?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "function alert(t) { return t >= 24 ? \"alert\" : \"normal\"; }\nfor (const t of [23,24,25]) print(t + \": \" + alert(t));", "extension": ["Read a program as a sequence of state changes. Before a loop begins, identify the inputs and initial variable values. At each iteration, record the current element, the condition's result, and any updated accumulator. After the loop, compare the returned value with the requirement. This manual trace can expose a problem even before the code is run, especially when a return statement appears in the wrong place.", "Collections and procedures let one solution apply to many examples. An array groups related values in order; a parameter lets a function receive a different collection or limit. Avoid copying an entire function just to change one threshold. Instead, choose a parameter name that explains its role and test several calls. A clear procedure contract states accepted inputs, output meaning, and behavior for unusual cases such as an empty collection.", "Testing is an investigation rather than a ceremony. Select cases that could reveal different failures. An empty collection tests initialization and the no-iteration path. A boundary value tests the exact comparison. A negative number tests an assumption that all values are positive. An invalid type tests whether the input contract is enforced. Write expected results independently, then run and compare. If a case fails, use the trace to locate the first point where behavior differs from the plan.", "JavaScript syntax and program logic are separate concerns. A missing brace can stop code from running, while an incorrect comparison can run successfully and still answer the wrong question. Fix the actual defect, keep a record of the evidence, and rerun earlier tests. Our coding window is temporary; preserve code in your response or assigned document. Use the output to support a claim about tested behavior, not as proof that every possible input will work.", "Use output deliberately while debugging. Print an intermediate value to check a hypothesis about the program, then compare it with the manual trace. A stream of unexplained output can make a defect harder to locate. Label the input and result so another reader knows which test produced each line. If the program fails to run, read the reported location and inspect nearby syntax before changing unrelated logic. If it runs with an incorrect answer, check the requirement, conditions, loop updates, and return position. The distinction helps you make a small justified repair instead of rewriting code without understanding the cause."], "support": "Revisit this example: Requirement: alert at 24 or higher. Code if(t>24) misses 24. Tests 23 and 25 pass, but test 24 fails. Replacing > with >= fixes the boundary, and rerunning all three tests checks the revision. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Add an invalid input policy and explain the valid solution range. Distinguish a logical error from a program that cannot execute. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w5d4", "week": 5, "day": 4, "title": "Loops and reusable procedures", "standards": ["ATP.CS.8.a", "ATP.M.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.c"], "reading": ["A loop repeats a sequence. A for-of loop visits each value in a collection. A counter or accumulator stores a result across iterations. Initialize it before the loop, update it inside the loop, and return it after the loop. Returning too early can prevent later values from being examined.", "A parameter is a name used by a procedure for an input value. An argument is the value supplied in a call. In above(readings,20), the collection and threshold are arguments. A parameterized procedure can solve several related cases without copying its entire body.", "Decomposition separates responsibilities. One function can count matches; another can format a report. Test the counting function separately from the report. A useful procedure has a clear input contract, predictable output, and a name that describes its purpose."], "example": "function countAbove(values,limit){let count=0; for(const value of values){if(value>limit)count++;} return count;} Calling countAbove([18,23,25],20) returns 2. Calling it with limit 24 returns 1 without changing the function body.", "tasks": ["Write countAbove(values,limit) and call it with two arrays and three thresholds.", "Write a second procedure to format the result. Explain how the subproblems connect.", "Move return inside the loop as a deliberate bug. Find a test that exposes it, repair it, and rerun your full test set."], "exit": "What makes a parameterized function more reusable than a copied code block?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["Read a program as a sequence of state changes. Before a loop begins, identify the inputs and initial variable values. At each iteration, record the current element, the condition's result, and any updated accumulator. After the loop, compare the returned value with the requirement. This manual trace can expose a problem even before the code is run, especially when a return statement appears in the wrong place.", "Collections and procedures let one solution apply to many examples. An array groups related values in order; a parameter lets a function receive a different collection or limit. Avoid copying an entire function just to change one threshold. Instead, choose a parameter name that explains its role and test several calls. A clear procedure contract states accepted inputs, output meaning, and behavior for unusual cases such as an empty collection.", "Testing is an investigation rather than a ceremony. Select cases that could reveal different failures. An empty collection tests initialization and the no-iteration path. A boundary value tests the exact comparison. A negative number tests an assumption that all values are positive. An invalid type tests whether the input contract is enforced. Write expected results independently, then run and compare. If a case fails, use the trace to locate the first point where behavior differs from the plan.", "JavaScript syntax and program logic are separate concerns. A missing brace can stop code from running, while an incorrect comparison can run successfully and still answer the wrong question. Fix the actual defect, keep a record of the evidence, and rerun earlier tests. Our coding window is temporary; preserve code in your response or assigned document. Use the output to support a claim about tested behavior, not as proof that every possible input will work.", "Use output deliberately while debugging. Print an intermediate value to check a hypothesis about the program, then compare it with the manual trace. A stream of unexplained output can make a defect harder to locate. Label the input and result so another reader knows which test produced each line. If the program fails to run, read the reported location and inspect nearby syntax before changing unrelated logic. If it runs with an incorrect answer, check the requirement, conditions, loop updates, and return position. The distinction helps you make a small justified repair instead of rewriting code without understanding the cause."], "support": "Revisit this example: function countAbove(values,limit){let count=0; for(const value of values){if(value>limit)count++;} return count;} Calling countAbove([18,23,25],20) returns 2. Calling it with limit 24 returns 1 without changing the function body. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Move return inside the loop as a deliberate bug. Find a test that exposes it, repair it, and rerun your full test set. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w5d5", "week": 5, "day": 5, "title": "Ship a tested data program", "standards": ["ATP.PD.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.b", "ATP.PD.8.c", "ATP.VDR.8.b"], "reading": ["Program development includes requirements, a plan, implementation, testing, and revision. A working example is a starting point. A robust program also behaves as specified for empty input, boundary values, and unusual valid cases. Invalid data needs a defined policy rather than an accidental result.", "A test record includes input, expected output, actual output, and pass or fail. Set the expected output before running the code. Otherwise you may simply accept what the program happens to do. After fixing a defect, rerun earlier tests to check that a revision did not break a previously correct case.", "Documentation explains the program's purpose, input contract, procedures, and limitations. Credit any source or collaborator. A classmate should be able to run your program from your instructions. The submitted artifact includes code and evidence, not only a claim that the program works."], "example": "For countAbove([],20), expected output is 0. For [20,21] at 20, expected output is 1. For [-2,0,3] at 0, expected output is 1. These tests exercise different conditions instead of repeating the same kind of positive list.", "tasks": ["Build a program that reports count and mean for a fictional numeric collection. Use a loop, decision, array, and two procedures, including one with parameters.", "Document five tests including empty, negative, boundary, and ordinary inputs. Define the mean output for empty input explicitly.", "Find and repair one defect, record before and after results, and submit code, tests, attribution, and limitations. Complete Week 5 test and the assigned mid-quarter benchmark."], "exit": "Why must expected outputs be written before running tests?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["Read a program as a sequence of state changes. Before a loop begins, identify the inputs and initial variable values. At each iteration, record the current element, the condition's result, and any updated accumulator. After the loop, compare the returned value with the requirement. This manual trace can expose a problem even before the code is run, especially when a return statement appears in the wrong place.", "Collections and procedures let one solution apply to many examples. An array groups related values in order; a parameter lets a function receive a different collection or limit. Avoid copying an entire function just to change one threshold. Instead, choose a parameter name that explains its role and test several calls. A clear procedure contract states accepted inputs, output meaning, and behavior for unusual cases such as an empty collection.", "Testing is an investigation rather than a ceremony. Select cases that could reveal different failures. An empty collection tests initialization and the no-iteration path. A boundary value tests the exact comparison. A negative number tests an assumption that all values are positive. An invalid type tests whether the input contract is enforced. Write expected results independently, then run and compare. If a case fails, use the trace to locate the first point where behavior differs from the plan.", "JavaScript syntax and program logic are separate concerns. A missing brace can stop code from running, while an incorrect comparison can run successfully and still answer the wrong question. Fix the actual defect, keep a record of the evidence, and rerun earlier tests. Our coding window is temporary; preserve code in your response or assigned document. Use the output to support a claim about tested behavior, not as proof that every possible input will work.", "Use output deliberately while debugging. Print an intermediate value to check a hypothesis about the program, then compare it with the manual trace. A stream of unexplained output can make a defect harder to locate. Label the input and result so another reader knows which test produced each line. If the program fails to run, read the reported location and inspect nearby syntax before changing unrelated logic. If it runs with an incorrect answer, check the requirement, conditions, loop updates, and return position. The distinction helps you make a small justified repair instead of rewriting code without understanding the cause."], "support": "Revisit this example: For countAbove([],20), expected output is 0. For [20,21] at 20, expected output is 1. For [-2,0,3] at 0, expected output is 1. These tests exercise different conditions instead of repeating the same kind of positive list. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Find and repair one defect, record before and after results, and submit code, tests, attribution, and limitations. Complete Week 5 test and the assigned mid-quarter benchmark. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Virtual robotics and algorithms", "goal": "Program a browser robot and justify efficient, tested solutions without physical equipment.", "lessons": [{"id": "w6d1", "week": 6, "day": 1, "title": "A robot needs precise instructions", "standards": ["ATP.A.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.a", "CS.HS.8.a"], "reading": ["A robot combines sensing, computation, and action. In our virtual world, a position and direction represent the robot's state. F moves one cell forward, L turns left, and R turns right. A turn changes direction without changing position. The environment has walls and a goal.", "Software commands have meaning only because a hardware or simulated actuator carries them out. Our model replaces motors with a grid update and replaces a distance sensor with a clear-path check. It does not model wheel slip, battery limits, or physical inertia. Explain those limits when making claims about real robots.", "Plan before running. Trace position and direction after every instruction. A route that looks short on the screen may fail if the robot faces the wrong way. An algorithm should specify both the initial state and the intended final state."], "example": "Start (0,0), east. F gives (1,0), east. R gives (1,0), south. F gives (1,1), south. The right turn did not move the robot to another cell.", "tasks": ["Trace F,R,F,L,F from the initial state, recording position and direction after every step.", "Use Robot Lab to test the trace. Explain any mismatch between prediction and result.", "Design a route to (4,4) and identify the simulated input, processing, and output components."], "exit": "Why is direction part of the robot's state?", "lab": "robot", "starter": "F\nR\nF\nL\nF", "extension": ["A virtual robot is useful because it makes state and rules visible. Record its coordinates, orientation, and the environment before evaluating a command. The same F instruction has different effects when the robot faces east or south. A blocked movement does not mean the entire algorithm is meaningless; it gives evidence that the route or safety check needs revision at a particular state.", "Separate planning from execution. A route diagram describes intended movement, pseudocode describes the decision process, and the command list tells the interpreter what to execute. Compare all three when debugging. If the diagram avoids a wall but the program reaches it, a translation or orientation error may have occurred. If both the plan and program enter a wall, revise the route itself. Record the location of the first mismatch.", "Define efficiency before comparing solutions. Source lines measure how much code is written. Executed instructions measure interpreter work. Forward moves measure distance in this simplified world. Real robots also consume energy while turning and may spend time sensing or correcting motion, which our model omits. A loop can improve readability without reducing physical travel. State the metric and assumptions every time you call one route better.", "The robot language intentionally has limits. REPEAT accepts a number and one basic command; it does not define custom procedures. IF CLEAR F checks only the next cell; it does not perform a general path search. Use JavaScript for richer procedures and explain how your abstraction relates to the grid. A careful report identifies both a demonstrated success and a feature the model cannot test, such as physical motor accuracy or unreliable sensors.", "When you communicate a route, define the coordinate system. Our grid has x increasing to the right and y increasing downward, with the initial robot facing east. A reader using a different convention could draw a different route from the same numbers. State whether turns count as actions and whether blocked commands stop execution. These definitions are part of the algorithm contract. They allow a reviewer to reproduce your results and decide whether another solution is actually comparable. If you move to a different simulator, check its coordinate and command rules before assuming your program will behave the same way."], "support": "Revisit this example: Start (0,0), east. F gives (1,0), east. R gives (1,0), south. F gives (1,1), south. The right turn did not move the robot to another cell. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Design a route to (4,4) and identify the simulated input, processing, and output components. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w6d2", "week": 6, "day": 2, "title": "Loops reduce repetition", "standards": ["ATP.CS.8.a", "ATP.A.8.a"], "reading": ["Repeated instructions can be expressed with a loop. REPEAT 4 F means perform F four times. It reduces the number of written lines but still executes four forward actions. Program length and executed work are different measures.", "A loop must repeat the correct unit. Repeating F then R four times traces a different route from moving forward four times and then turning once. Our simple robot language permits only one repeated command per REPEAT line. More complex loops belong in JavaScript, where braces define a repeated block.", "Compare algorithms using a clear metric. For this grid, count forward moves and executed steps. A compact program can still take a long route. Test both the final position and whether the robot collides with a wall; reaching a location in your imagination is not enough."], "example": "F,F,F,F,R,F,F,F,F and REPEAT 4 F; R; REPEAT 4 F both reach (4,4) from the initial state. Both execute eight forwards and one turn. The second is shorter to write, but it does not reduce the movement distance.", "tasks": ["Write expanded and repeated-command versions of a route along the top and right edges.", "Run both programs. Compare line count, executed steps, forward moves, and final state.", "Propose a misleading efficiency claim, then correct it using your measured results."], "exit": "Does replacing four F lines with REPEAT 4 F reduce the number of forward movements?", "lab": "robot", "starter": "", "extension": ["A virtual robot is useful because it makes state and rules visible. Record its coordinates, orientation, and the environment before evaluating a command. The same F instruction has different effects when the robot faces east or south. A blocked movement does not mean the entire algorithm is meaningless; it gives evidence that the route or safety check needs revision at a particular state.", "Separate planning from execution. A route diagram describes intended movement, pseudocode describes the decision process, and the command list tells the interpreter what to execute. Compare all three when debugging. If the diagram avoids a wall but the program reaches it, a translation or orientation error may have occurred. If both the plan and program enter a wall, revise the route itself. Record the location of the first mismatch.", "Define efficiency before comparing solutions. Source lines measure how much code is written. Executed instructions measure interpreter work. Forward moves measure distance in this simplified world. Real robots also consume energy while turning and may spend time sensing or correcting motion, which our model omits. A loop can improve readability without reducing physical travel. State the metric and assumptions every time you call one route better.", "The robot language intentionally has limits. REPEAT accepts a number and one basic command; it does not define custom procedures. IF CLEAR F checks only the next cell; it does not perform a general path search. Use JavaScript for richer procedures and explain how your abstraction relates to the grid. A careful report identifies both a demonstrated success and a feature the model cannot test, such as physical motor accuracy or unreliable sensors.", "When you communicate a route, define the coordinate system. Our grid has x increasing to the right and y increasing downward, with the initial robot facing east. A reader using a different convention could draw a different route from the same numbers. State whether turns count as actions and whether blocked commands stop execution. These definitions are part of the algorithm contract. They allow a reviewer to reproduce your results and decide whether another solution is actually comparable. If you move to a different simulator, check its coordinate and command rules before assuming your program will behave the same way."], "support": "Revisit this example: F,F,F,F,R,F,F,F,F and REPEAT 4 F; R; REPEAT 4 F both reach (4,4) from the initial state. Both execute eight forwards and one turn. The second is shorter to write, but it does not reduce the movement distance. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Propose a misleading efficiency claim, then correct it using your measured results. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w6d3", "week": 6, "day": 3, "title": "Sense decide act", "standards": ["ATP.CS.8.a", "DA.IM.8.a"], "reading": ["A conditional uses a sensed condition to decide what to do. IF CLEAR F moves only when the next cell is open. A blocked unconditional F stops our model. This difference lets a program avoid one kind of collision, but it does not automatically find a route to the goal.", "A sensor model has limits. Our check is exact and considers only the cell directly ahead. Real sensors can be noisy or incomplete. A safe design considers what to do when a reading is unavailable or uncertain. A conditional is only as useful as the condition and response the programmer chooses.", "Test conditions in both states. A program tested only on a clear path has not demonstrated how it behaves at a wall. Boundary cells also matter: moving off the grid is blocked. Compare the expected behavior with the actual result and explain what the test supports."], "example": "At (1,1) facing east, the cell (2,1) is a wall. IF CLEAR F leaves the robot at (1,1); it does not turn or move around the wall. A following R command is needed if the algorithm calls for a new direction.", "tasks": ["Create a route that reaches (1,1) facing east. Compare F with IF CLEAR F at that location.", "Test a clear cell, a wall, and the grid boundary. Record outcomes for each.", "Write pseudocode for a sense-decide-act controller that could explore safely. State which features our command language cannot express."], "exit": "Why does avoiding one collision not prove that an algorithm finds the goal?", "lab": "robot", "starter": "F\nR\nF\nL\nIF CLEAR F", "extension": ["A virtual robot is useful because it makes state and rules visible. Record its coordinates, orientation, and the environment before evaluating a command. The same F instruction has different effects when the robot faces east or south. A blocked movement does not mean the entire algorithm is meaningless; it gives evidence that the route or safety check needs revision at a particular state.", "Separate planning from execution. A route diagram describes intended movement, pseudocode describes the decision process, and the command list tells the interpreter what to execute. Compare all three when debugging. If the diagram avoids a wall but the program reaches it, a translation or orientation error may have occurred. If both the plan and program enter a wall, revise the route itself. Record the location of the first mismatch.", "Define efficiency before comparing solutions. Source lines measure how much code is written. Executed instructions measure interpreter work. Forward moves measure distance in this simplified world. Real robots also consume energy while turning and may spend time sensing or correcting motion, which our model omits. A loop can improve readability without reducing physical travel. State the metric and assumptions every time you call one route better.", "The robot language intentionally has limits. REPEAT accepts a number and one basic command; it does not define custom procedures. IF CLEAR F checks only the next cell; it does not perform a general path search. Use JavaScript for richer procedures and explain how your abstraction relates to the grid. A careful report identifies both a demonstrated success and a feature the model cannot test, such as physical motor accuracy or unreliable sensors.", "When you communicate a route, define the coordinate system. Our grid has x increasing to the right and y increasing downward, with the initial robot facing east. A reader using a different convention could draw a different route from the same numbers. State whether turns count as actions and whether blocked commands stop execution. These definitions are part of the algorithm contract. They allow a reviewer to reproduce your results and decide whether another solution is actually comparable. If you move to a different simulator, check its coordinate and command rules before assuming your program will behave the same way."], "support": "Revisit this example: At (1,1) facing east, the cell (2,1) is a wall. IF CLEAR F leaves the robot at (1,1); it does not turn or move around the wall. A following R command is needed if the algorithm calls for a new direction. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write pseudocode for a sense-decide-act controller that could explore safely. State which features our command language cannot express. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w6d4", "week": 6, "day": 4, "title": "Decompose a robot controller", "standards": ["ATP.M.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.c", "ATP.PD.8.b"], "reading": ["A route can be decomposed into segments and decisions. In a richer program, a procedure such as move(n) accepts a distance parameter and repeats a movement operation. A second procedure can turn or check safety. Decomposition makes each responsibility testable.", "Our Robot Lab deliberately uses a small command language. It supports repeated commands but not user-defined procedures. Use Coding Lab to model reusable procedures numerically, then translate the resulting route into Robot Lab commands. Explain this translation rather than claiming the robot interpreter supports a feature it does not.", "A test should verify the procedure's contract. For move(n), specify valid nonnegative integer values and reject invalid distances. Test zero as well as positive values. A procedure that works only for n=4 is not yet demonstrated to work across its stated valid range."], "example": "JavaScript model: function move(x,n){if(!Number.isInteger(n)||n<0) throw Error(\"invalid distance\"); return x+n;} Tests move(0,0)=0 and move(0,4)=4 verify two distances. The model omits walls, so it cannot certify that a physical path is clear.", "tasks": ["Write move(position,distance) and a separate report procedure in Coding Lab. Pass at least two parameter values.", "Test zero, positive, negative, and fractional distances against your contract.", "Translate a valid result into Robot Lab commands by visiting yesterday's lesson. Explain the abstraction and one feature it leaves out."], "exit": "Why must a numeric movement model state that it omits walls?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "function move(x,n) {\n if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) throw Error(\"invalid distance\");\n return x+n;\n}\nprint(move(0,0));\nprint(move(0,4));", "extension": ["A virtual robot is useful because it makes state and rules visible. Record its coordinates, orientation, and the environment before evaluating a command. The same F instruction has different effects when the robot faces east or south. A blocked movement does not mean the entire algorithm is meaningless; it gives evidence that the route or safety check needs revision at a particular state.", "Separate planning from execution. A route diagram describes intended movement, pseudocode describes the decision process, and the command list tells the interpreter what to execute. Compare all three when debugging. If the diagram avoids a wall but the program reaches it, a translation or orientation error may have occurred. If both the plan and program enter a wall, revise the route itself. Record the location of the first mismatch.", "Define efficiency before comparing solutions. Source lines measure how much code is written. Executed instructions measure interpreter work. Forward moves measure distance in this simplified world. Real robots also consume energy while turning and may spend time sensing or correcting motion, which our model omits. A loop can improve readability without reducing physical travel. State the metric and assumptions every time you call one route better.", "The robot language intentionally has limits. REPEAT accepts a number and one basic command; it does not define custom procedures. IF CLEAR F checks only the next cell; it does not perform a general path search. Use JavaScript for richer procedures and explain how your abstraction relates to the grid. A careful report identifies both a demonstrated success and a feature the model cannot test, such as physical motor accuracy or unreliable sensors.", "When you communicate a route, define the coordinate system. Our grid has x increasing to the right and y increasing downward, with the initial robot facing east. A reader using a different convention could draw a different route from the same numbers. State whether turns count as actions and whether blocked commands stop execution. These definitions are part of the algorithm contract. They allow a reviewer to reproduce your results and decide whether another solution is actually comparable. If you move to a different simulator, check its coordinate and command rules before assuming your program will behave the same way."], "support": "Revisit this example: JavaScript model: function move(x,n){if(!Number.isInteger(n)||n<0) throw Error(\"invalid distance\"); return x+n;} Tests move(0,0)=0 and move(0,4)=4 verify two distances. The model omits walls, so it cannot certify that a physical path is clear. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Translate a valid result into Robot Lab commands by visiting yesterday's lesson. Explain the abstraction and one feature it leaves out. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w6d5", "week": 6, "day": 5, "title": "Robot mission and design review", "standards": ["ATP.A.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.b", "CS.HS.8.a"], "reading": ["A mission report connects a requirement to code and evidence. The requirement here is to reach the goal without a blocked movement. Your report should state the initial state, route, commands, measured actions, and final state. Compare alternatives with the same success criteria.", "A route can be valid without being the only valid route. To justify efficiency, explain your metric and lower bound. From (0,0) to (4,4), any route using only orthogonal forward movements requires at least eight cell movements because four horizontal and four vertical changes are necessary. Obstacles may force additional movements for some routes.", "A review also considers accessibility and usability. Can another student read the commands, understand errors, and reproduce the result using keyboard controls? Include one revision based on a review. A clean result is stronger when another person can verify how it was obtained."], "example": "The top-edge then right-edge route takes eight forward moves and one turn, avoiding the internal walls. Eight forwards meet the coordinate lower bound, so this route is movement-optimal under our stated grid rules. It is not proof about real robot energy use.", "tasks": ["Create two valid routes to (4,4). Record code, final state, forward moves, and executed steps.", "Justify a preferred route using a metric and the coordinate lower bound. Name one metric your evidence does not measure.", "Complete a digital peer or self-review and revise the instructions. Submit your mission report and complete the Week 6 test."], "exit": "Under which assumptions can you call your route optimal?", "lab": "robot", "starter": "", "extension": ["A virtual robot is useful because it makes state and rules visible. Record its coordinates, orientation, and the environment before evaluating a command. The same F instruction has different effects when the robot faces east or south. A blocked movement does not mean the entire algorithm is meaningless; it gives evidence that the route or safety check needs revision at a particular state.", "Separate planning from execution. A route diagram describes intended movement, pseudocode describes the decision process, and the command list tells the interpreter what to execute. Compare all three when debugging. If the diagram avoids a wall but the program reaches it, a translation or orientation error may have occurred. If both the plan and program enter a wall, revise the route itself. Record the location of the first mismatch.", "Define efficiency before comparing solutions. Source lines measure how much code is written. Executed instructions measure interpreter work. Forward moves measure distance in this simplified world. Real robots also consume energy while turning and may spend time sensing or correcting motion, which our model omits. A loop can improve readability without reducing physical travel. State the metric and assumptions every time you call one route better.", "The robot language intentionally has limits. REPEAT accepts a number and one basic command; it does not define custom procedures. IF CLEAR F checks only the next cell; it does not perform a general path search. Use JavaScript for richer procedures and explain how your abstraction relates to the grid. A careful report identifies both a demonstrated success and a feature the model cannot test, such as physical motor accuracy or unreliable sensors.", "When you communicate a route, define the coordinate system. Our grid has x increasing to the right and y increasing downward, with the initial robot facing east. A reader using a different convention could draw a different route from the same numbers. State whether turns count as actions and whether blocked commands stop execution. These definitions are part of the algorithm contract. They allow a reviewer to reproduce your results and decide whether another solution is actually comparable. If you move to a different simulator, check its coordinate and command rules before assuming your program will behave the same way."], "support": "Revisit this example: The top-edge then right-edge route takes eight forward moves and one turn, avoiding the internal walls. Eight forwards meet the coordinate lower bound, so this route is movement-optimal under our stated grid rules. It is not proof about real robot energy use. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Complete a digital peer or self-review and revise the instructions. Submit your mission report and complete the Week 6 test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "AI perception and reasoning", "goal": "Represent images and sound, build context rules, and trace breadth-first graph search.", "lessons": [{"id": "w7d1", "week": 7, "day": 1, "title": "What counts as intelligence?", "standards": ["AI.NI.8.b", "IC.Cu.8.d"], "reading": ["Intelligence includes many abilities: adapting, learning, recognizing patterns, reasoning, communicating, and acting toward goals. Humans, animals, and machines show different combinations. A machine can excel at a narrow task without understanding the world as a person does.", "A system's convincing output is not direct evidence of awareness, feelings, or human understanding. Evaluate an operational claim with a measurable task. For a route finder, test whether it finds a valid path. For a classifier, test predictions on examples not used for training. Neither test measures every possible meaning of intelligence.", "Computing changes work in other fields. Pattern recognition can support agriculture or medicine, while mistakes can also create harm. A responsible design identifies who uses the result, who checks it, and what happens when the system is wrong. Human review is especially important when consequences are significant."], "example": "A calculator multiplies large numbers faster than most people but cannot decide what question a worried friend needs answered. A dog may navigate a familiar environment flexibly without solving arithmetic. Compare abilities by task instead of ranking all intelligence on one scale.", "tasks": ["Compare human, animal, and machine abilities for three specific tasks. State the evidence you would need.", "Use Search Lab to observe a machine-style reasoning procedure. Explain what it does and what it does not show about understanding.", "Propose a computing application in agriculture or another field. Explain a benefit, failure risk, and human review step."], "exit": "Why is success on one task insufficient to establish human-like intelligence?", "lab": "search", "starter": "", "extension": ["Artificial intelligence systems combine representations, algorithms, and evidence. A representation determines which features of a situation are available to an algorithm. A pixel grid preserves selected image information; sound samples preserve selected measurements over time; a graph preserves relationships among nodes. Each representation leaves things out. Ask whether the omitted detail matters for the task before treating the model's output as a complete account of reality.", "Perception and reasoning can involve several stages. A sensor produces measurements, software stores numbers, a feature-extraction process identifies patterns, and a later process chooses a label or action. An error can enter at any stage. Blurry pixels, ambiguous words, narrow examples, or an unsuitable rule can each contribute to a wrong result. Distinguishing the stages helps you propose a specific improvement rather than saying only that the AI is bad.", "A search algorithm also relies on representation. In a graph, nodes and edges stand for chosen objects and relationships. A breadth-first search explores by edge distance and records first-discovery parents. If an edge represents a trip with a different travel time from another edge, fewest edges and fastest route can differ. If a connection is missing from the graph, the search cannot discover it by reasoning harder. The input model limits the result.", "Inclusive design asks whose objects, language, environments, and needs are represented. Consult different perspectives, create clear labels, examine relevant failure cases, and explain a fallback. A classroom model should make its method visible and permit criticism. Producing an answer is only one part of a useful system; explaining what the answer means, how it was obtained, and when it might fail is part of responsible computing.", "Use an explicit record when tracing an algorithm. A table can show step number, current input, stored state, and output. For image interpretation, record the region and chosen label. For phrase matching, record the normalized message and first matching rule. For graph search, record the removed node, newly discovered neighbors, and updated queue. These records make the process inspectable and help identify where a wrong result began. They also show what the algorithm actually considered. An impressive final answer without a trace gives less evidence about the method and is harder for another student to verify or improve independently."], "support": "Revisit this example: A calculator multiplies large numbers faster than most people but cannot decide what question a worried friend needs answered. A dog may navigate a familiar environment flexibly without solving arithmetic. Compare abilities by task instead of ranking all intelligence on one scale. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Propose a computing application in agriculture or another field. Explain a benefit, failure risk, and human review step. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w7d2", "week": 7, "day": 2, "title": "Pixels samples and sensor data", "standards": ["AI.P.8.a", "DA.DCS.8.b"], "reading": ["An image can be stored as a grid of pixels. Each pixel has numeric values representing color or brightness. A grayscale image might use one value per pixel; an RGB image uses red, green, and blue components. Resolution describes the number of pixels, while the possible values affect how much detail each pixel can represent.", "Sound is a changing pressure signal. A digital recording samples the signal at discrete times and stores numbers for the measured amplitudes. A sample rate describes how often measurements are taken. Quantization maps measurements into a finite set of numeric values. More detail can require more storage.", "A computer's perception system uses those numbers to extract features and infer meaning. Stored samples are not the same as understanding a song or sentence. Noise, missing detail, or limited training examples can affect the later interpretation. Our small models help trace representation without using a camera or microphone."], "example": "A 2 by 2 grayscale image [0,255;255,0] uses four brightness values to show a checker pattern. A toy sound [0,2,0,-2] records four amplitudes at equal time steps. The array values alone need an order and timing rule to reconstruct the intended signal.", "tasks": ["Create a 4 by 4 grayscale image as a numeric table. Explain how a reader reconstructs it.", "Represent a short fictional sound using eight amplitude samples at equal intervals. Explain what changes if you keep only every other sample.", "Use the AI region map to label areas of a toy image. Describe the difference between raw values and an interpreted object label."], "exit": "How do pixel values and sound samples differ from the meanings inferred from them?", "lab": "ai", "starter": "", "extension": ["Artificial intelligence systems combine representations, algorithms, and evidence. A representation determines which features of a situation are available to an algorithm. A pixel grid preserves selected image information; sound samples preserve selected measurements over time; a graph preserves relationships among nodes. Each representation leaves things out. Ask whether the omitted detail matters for the task before treating the model's output as a complete account of reality.", "Perception and reasoning can involve several stages. A sensor produces measurements, software stores numbers, a feature-extraction process identifies patterns, and a later process chooses a label or action. An error can enter at any stage. Blurry pixels, ambiguous words, narrow examples, or an unsuitable rule can each contribute to a wrong result. Distinguishing the stages helps you propose a specific improvement rather than saying only that the AI is bad.", "A search algorithm also relies on representation. In a graph, nodes and edges stand for chosen objects and relationships. A breadth-first search explores by edge distance and records first-discovery parents. If an edge represents a trip with a different travel time from another edge, fewest edges and fastest route can differ. If a connection is missing from the graph, the search cannot discover it by reasoning harder. The input model limits the result.", "Inclusive design asks whose objects, language, environments, and needs are represented. Consult different perspectives, create clear labels, examine relevant failure cases, and explain a fallback. A classroom model should make its method visible and permit criticism. Producing an answer is only one part of a useful system; explaining what the answer means, how it was obtained, and when it might fail is part of responsible computing.", "Use an explicit record when tracing an algorithm. A table can show step number, current input, stored state, and output. For image interpretation, record the region and chosen label. For phrase matching, record the normalized message and first matching rule. For graph search, record the removed node, newly discovered neighbors, and updated queue. These records make the process inspectable and help identify where a wrong result began. They also show what the algorithm actually considered. An impressive final answer without a trace gives less evidence about the method and is harder for another student to verify or improve independently."], "support": "Revisit this example: A 2 by 2 grayscale image [0,255;255,0] uses four brightness values to show a checker pattern. A toy sound [0,2,0,-2] records four amplitudes at equal time steps. The array values alone need an order and timing rule to reconstruct the intended signal. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Use the AI region map to label areas of a toy image. Describe the difference between raw values and an interpreted object label. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w7d3", "week": 7, "day": 3, "title": "Context can repair ambiguity", "standards": ["AI.P.8.c", "AI.NI.8.a"], "reading": ["A word or symbol can be ambiguous. Context helps an interpreter choose among possible meanings. In “open the file,” file likely means a digital document; in a workshop it might mean a hand tool. A text recognition system can combine uncertain character evidence with likely word sequences.", "Optical character recognition detects text in an image. Language processing examines text sequences. These are related stages but different tasks. Our phrase matcher starts with typed text, so it models a small language-processing stage rather than performing real image-based OCR.", "Rules can use multiword phrases to avoid overly broad matches. Put a specific phrase before a general one when the program chooses the first match. Include a fallback for unknown requests. Test paraphrases, punctuation, and a misleading phrase. A successful example does not establish that all natural language will be understood."], "example": "Rules for “reset password” and “password” overlap. If the broad password rule runs first, it may hide the reset response. Testing “Can I reset password?” exposes the issue. A separate fallback handles “I cannot access my lesson” until a suitable rule is added.", "tasks": ["In Chatbot Lab create specific and broad phrase rules. Reverse their order and compare results.", "Create five ambiguous or partially unclear phrases. For each, explain which context words help interpret it.", "Add rules and a fallback to form a text recognition decision model. Test two paraphrases it misses and explain the limitation."], "exit": "Why should a specific phrase sometimes be checked before a general word?", "lab": "chatbot", "starter": "", "extension": ["Artificial intelligence systems combine representations, algorithms, and evidence. A representation determines which features of a situation are available to an algorithm. A pixel grid preserves selected image information; sound samples preserve selected measurements over time; a graph preserves relationships among nodes. Each representation leaves things out. Ask whether the omitted detail matters for the task before treating the model's output as a complete account of reality.", "Perception and reasoning can involve several stages. A sensor produces measurements, software stores numbers, a feature-extraction process identifies patterns, and a later process chooses a label or action. An error can enter at any stage. Blurry pixels, ambiguous words, narrow examples, or an unsuitable rule can each contribute to a wrong result. Distinguishing the stages helps you propose a specific improvement rather than saying only that the AI is bad.", "A search algorithm also relies on representation. In a graph, nodes and edges stand for chosen objects and relationships. A breadth-first search explores by edge distance and records first-discovery parents. If an edge represents a trip with a different travel time from another edge, fewest edges and fastest route can differ. If a connection is missing from the graph, the search cannot discover it by reasoning harder. The input model limits the result.", "Inclusive design asks whose objects, language, environments, and needs are represented. Consult different perspectives, create clear labels, examine relevant failure cases, and explain a fallback. A classroom model should make its method visible and permit criticism. Producing an answer is only one part of a useful system; explaining what the answer means, how it was obtained, and when it might fail is part of responsible computing.", "Use an explicit record when tracing an algorithm. A table can show step number, current input, stored state, and output. For image interpretation, record the region and chosen label. For phrase matching, record the normalized message and first matching rule. For graph search, record the removed node, newly discovered neighbors, and updated queue. These records make the process inspectable and help identify where a wrong result began. They also show what the algorithm actually considered. An impressive final answer without a trace gives less evidence about the method and is harder for another student to verify or improve independently."], "support": "Revisit this example: Rules for “reset password” and “password” overlap. If the broad password rule runs first, it may hide the reset response. Testing “Can I reset password?” exposes the issue. A separate fallback handles “I cannot access my lesson” until a suitable rule is added. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Add rules and a fallback to form a text recognition decision model. Test two paraphrases it misses and explain the limitation. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w7d4", "week": 7, "day": 4, "title": "Breadth-first search", "standards": ["AI.RR.8.a", "ATP.VDR.8.b"], "reading": ["A graph represents objects as nodes and relationships as edges. A search tree records how a search first discovers nodes from a starting point. Breadth-first search explores all nodes at one edge distance before moving farther away. It uses a queue: first in, first out.", "Start by marking the initial node as discovered and putting it in the queue. Remove the first node, inspect its neighbors in a stated order, and add any undiscovered neighbors to the end. Marking on discovery avoids adding the same node repeatedly. Record the parent that first discovered each node.", "On an unweighted graph, breadth-first search finds a path with the fewest edges to any reachable node. It does not necessarily minimize travel time when different edges have different costs. The neighbor order can change the chosen tree when multiple shortest paths exist, so include that order in your trace."], "example": "Edges A-B,A-C,B-D,B-E,C-F,E-G,F-G. Start A. Expand A: queue B,C. Expand B: queue C,D,E. Expand C: queue D,E,F. E later discovers G. The parent chain A-B-E-G is a three-edge path. Marking G discovered prevents F from adding it again.", "tasks": ["Predict the full expansion order for Search Lab using alphabetical neighbors.", "Step through the lab. Record queue contents after each expansion and draw the resulting parent tree using text arrows.", "Find a path from A to G and justify its edge count. Explain how unequal travel costs could change the best route."], "exit": "Why does a queue support breadth-first exploration?", "lab": "search", "starter": "", "extension": ["Artificial intelligence systems combine representations, algorithms, and evidence. A representation determines which features of a situation are available to an algorithm. A pixel grid preserves selected image information; sound samples preserve selected measurements over time; a graph preserves relationships among nodes. Each representation leaves things out. Ask whether the omitted detail matters for the task before treating the model's output as a complete account of reality.", "Perception and reasoning can involve several stages. A sensor produces measurements, software stores numbers, a feature-extraction process identifies patterns, and a later process chooses a label or action. An error can enter at any stage. Blurry pixels, ambiguous words, narrow examples, or an unsuitable rule can each contribute to a wrong result. Distinguishing the stages helps you propose a specific improvement rather than saying only that the AI is bad.", "A search algorithm also relies on representation. In a graph, nodes and edges stand for chosen objects and relationships. A breadth-first search explores by edge distance and records first-discovery parents. If an edge represents a trip with a different travel time from another edge, fewest edges and fastest route can differ. If a connection is missing from the graph, the search cannot discover it by reasoning harder. The input model limits the result.", "Inclusive design asks whose objects, language, environments, and needs are represented. Consult different perspectives, create clear labels, examine relevant failure cases, and explain a fallback. A classroom model should make its method visible and permit criticism. Producing an answer is only one part of a useful system; explaining what the answer means, how it was obtained, and when it might fail is part of responsible computing.", "Use an explicit record when tracing an algorithm. A table can show step number, current input, stored state, and output. For image interpretation, record the region and chosen label. For phrase matching, record the normalized message and first matching rule. For graph search, record the removed node, newly discovered neighbors, and updated queue. These records make the process inspectable and help identify where a wrong result began. They also show what the algorithm actually considered. An impressive final answer without a trace gives less evidence about the method and is harder for another student to verify or improve independently."], "support": "Revisit this example: Edges A-B,A-C,B-D,B-E,C-F,E-G,F-G. Start A. Expand A: queue B,C. Expand B: queue C,D,E. Expand C: queue D,E,F. E later discovers G. The parent chain A-B-E-G is a three-edge path. Marking G discovered prevents F from adding it again. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Find a path from A to G and justify its edge count. Explain how unequal travel costs could change the best route. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w7d5", "week": 7, "day": 5, "title": "An inclusive perception design", "standards": ["AI.P.8.b", "IC.Cu.8.b", "AI.RR.8.a"], "reading": ["A vision system may perform poorly when its development data omits objects, styles, or settings from some cultures. A system trained only on one kind of cup may mislabel other forms of drinking vessel. The error can come from narrow representation rather than an object's actual category.", "Inclusive design begins by identifying intended users and contexts, involving people with different perspectives, and evaluating failures across relevant groups or conditions. Adding examples helps only when the labels, features, and evaluation process also fit the task. A balanced count of two labels does not guarantee cultural coverage.", "Write measurable design criteria. A criterion such as test objects from several usage contexts is more actionable than make it fair. Include a process for reporting errors and a way for people to override an inappropriate result. Accessibility also includes readable instructions and alternatives to visual-only feedback."], "example": "A classifier calls every handled vessel a cup and every handleless vessel a bowl. A handleless drinking cup exposes its weak feature assumption. A revision adds varied vessel examples, considers function and shape, and tests unseen objects from several contexts.", "tasks": ["Use AI Lab with a narrow training set and an unseen test value. Record the prediction and its limits.", "Propose an inclusive vision dataset with examples, labels, segmentation boundaries, and three evaluation conditions. Do not collect personal photographs.", "Submit a perception design review and a breadth-first trace. Explain a guideline that would reduce cultural bias and complete the Week 7 test."], "exit": "Why can equal label counts still leave important groups or contexts unrepresented?", "lab": "ai", "starter": "", "extension": ["Artificial intelligence systems combine representations, algorithms, and evidence. A representation determines which features of a situation are available to an algorithm. A pixel grid preserves selected image information; sound samples preserve selected measurements over time; a graph preserves relationships among nodes. Each representation leaves things out. Ask whether the omitted detail matters for the task before treating the model's output as a complete account of reality.", "Perception and reasoning can involve several stages. A sensor produces measurements, software stores numbers, a feature-extraction process identifies patterns, and a later process chooses a label or action. An error can enter at any stage. Blurry pixels, ambiguous words, narrow examples, or an unsuitable rule can each contribute to a wrong result. Distinguishing the stages helps you propose a specific improvement rather than saying only that the AI is bad.", "A search algorithm also relies on representation. In a graph, nodes and edges stand for chosen objects and relationships. A breadth-first search explores by edge distance and records first-discovery parents. If an edge represents a trip with a different travel time from another edge, fewest edges and fastest route can differ. If a connection is missing from the graph, the search cannot discover it by reasoning harder. The input model limits the result.", "Inclusive design asks whose objects, language, environments, and needs are represented. Consult different perspectives, create clear labels, examine relevant failure cases, and explain a fallback. A classroom model should make its method visible and permit criticism. Producing an answer is only one part of a useful system; explaining what the answer means, how it was obtained, and when it might fail is part of responsible computing.", "Use an explicit record when tracing an algorithm. A table can show step number, current input, stored state, and output. For image interpretation, record the region and chosen label. For phrase matching, record the normalized message and first matching rule. For graph search, record the removed node, newly discovered neighbors, and updated queue. These records make the process inspectable and help identify where a wrong result began. They also show what the algorithm actually considered. An impressive final answer without a trace gives less evidence about the method and is harder for another student to verify or improve independently."], "support": "Revisit this example: A classifier calls every handled vessel a cup and every handleless vessel a bowl. A handleless drinking cup exposes its weak feature assumption. A revision adds varied vessel examples, considers function and shape, and tests unseen objects from several contexts. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Submit a perception design review and a breadth-first trace. Explain a guideline that would reduce cultural bias and complete the Week 7 test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Machine learning and a helpful chatbot", "goal": "Train and test a tiny classifier, investigate bias, and build a functional text-processing chatbot.", "lessons": [{"id": "w8d1", "week": 8, "day": 1, "title": "Training is different from using", "standards": ["AI.ML.8.a", "AI.NI.8.a"], "reading": ["Training uses examples to establish a model's behavior. Using a trained model applies that behavior to a new input. In a supervised task, training examples include labels or target values. A classifier outputs a category such as cup or bowl. A numeric prediction model estimates a quantity such as temperature.", "Our nearest-neighbor classifier stores labeled numeric examples. For an unseen size it finds the closest training size and returns that example's label. This is a deliberately small learning method. It does not infer every useful property of an object, and its output depends strongly on the chosen feature and examples.", "Keep training and test examples separate. Reusing the same examples for both can make performance look better without showing how the model handles new cases. Changing the visible training text does not alter our stored model until Train is pressed. That distinction lets you observe training and inference as separate operations."], "example": "Train on (2,cup),(3,cup),(8,bowl). Test size 6: distance to 3 is 3; distance to 8 is 2, so predict bowl. Adding (6,cup) changes the training examples. After retraining, the same input predicts cup. The input did not become more intelligent; the model evidence changed.", "tasks": ["Train the default AI model and predict labels for sizes 1,4,6,9 before classifying.", "Edit a training row without retraining and test again. Then retrain and compare.", "Explain classification versus numeric prediction using the same fictional table. State which output our lab supports."], "exit": "What changes during training that does not change during one classification?", "lab": "ai", "starter": "", "extension": ["Machine learning work requires an explicit task and evaluation plan. Decide whether the desired output is a category or a number, select relevant input features, and define what a correct output means. A nearest-neighbor classifier can use numeric distance to select a category, but distance in one feature is only a useful shortcut if that feature relates to the task. Size alone cannot reliably capture every distinction between cups and bowls.", "A data set includes choices made by people. Which examples were collected, which categories were defined, how ambiguous examples were labeled, and which conditions were omitted all affect behavior. Repeating many similar examples may increase the row count while leaving important gaps. Adding diverse examples can help, but it does not automatically correct an unsuitable feature, inconsistent labels, or a poorly chosen evaluation measure.", "Separate model development from evaluation. Use training examples to establish behavior, then apply the stored model to held-out examples with known labels. Record each result rather than only a summary percentage. Compare kinds of mistakes and relevant conditions. After using a failure to guide a revision, reserve new examples for another check. This helps you distinguish correcting a known case from improving performance on unfamiliar cases.", "A rule-based chatbot provides a useful comparison. Its replies come from conditions written by a programmer rather than from training on the classifier's examples. It still processes language, and its phrase order, normalization, and fallback determine behavior. Document which messages you tested and which it misses. Keep all examples fictional, avoid collecting secrets, and provide a clear human help route. A limitation notice should describe the actual method and uncertainty rather than giving the system an exaggerated human personality.", "Document a dataset so another person can interpret it. Include the feature names, units where relevant, label definitions, collection method, intended task, and known gaps. Describe how examples were divided into training and evaluation sets. If a label is uncertain, explain the policy instead of hiding the disagreement. If examples represent only a narrow setting, say so in the model notice. A transparent dataset description does not guarantee a good model, but it helps a reviewer identify possible weaknesses and reproduce the investigation. It also prevents a future user from assuming that a tiny classroom dataset represents a broad real-world population."], "support": "Revisit this example: Train on (2,cup),(3,cup),(8,bowl). Test size 6: distance to 3 is 3; distance to 8 is 2, so predict bowl. Adding (6,cup) changes the training examples. After retraining, the same input predicts cup. The input did not become more intelligent; the model evidence changed. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Explain classification versus numeric prediction using the same fictional table. State which output our lab supports. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w8d2", "week": 8, "day": 2, "title": "Build a labeled training set", "standards": ["AI.ML.8.b", "AI.P.8.b"], "reading": ["Object recognition needs a clear description of what counts as an object and how it is labeled. Segmentation separates image regions belonging to different objects or background. Labeling assigns categories to those regions. A label for an entire image can be insufficient when the image contains several objects.", "A training set should include relevant variation: different sizes, shapes, orientations, backgrounds, and usage contexts. Consistent labels matter. If one annotator calls an object cup and another calls the same object bowl without a shared rule, the model receives conflicting evidence.", "Our region map is a toy representation, not an image recognition engine. You can assign regions to labels and record boundaries, but it does not train a vision model from those selections. Use it to demonstrate a labeling procedure and document the assumptions you would need before training a real system."], "example": "Imagine two adjacent objects in a 3 by 3 region map. Assign regions 1,2,4 to one cup and regions 6,8,9 to one bowl, leaving 3,5,7 as background. The region labels alone do not distinguish two different cups unless you also record object identifiers.", "tasks": ["Use the AI region map to create a labeled toy scene. Record object IDs, regions, category labels, and background regions.", "Write an annotation rule another student could apply consistently. Test it with a partner or a second-role review.", "Design a diverse twelve-example training set. Explain one source of cultural or context bias and how the set addresses it."], "exit": "Why are segmentation and category labeling different operations?", "lab": "ai", "starter": "", "extension": ["Machine learning work requires an explicit task and evaluation plan. Decide whether the desired output is a category or a number, select relevant input features, and define what a correct output means. A nearest-neighbor classifier can use numeric distance to select a category, but distance in one feature is only a useful shortcut if that feature relates to the task. Size alone cannot reliably capture every distinction between cups and bowls.", "A data set includes choices made by people. Which examples were collected, which categories were defined, how ambiguous examples were labeled, and which conditions were omitted all affect behavior. Repeating many similar examples may increase the row count while leaving important gaps. Adding diverse examples can help, but it does not automatically correct an unsuitable feature, inconsistent labels, or a poorly chosen evaluation measure.", "Separate model development from evaluation. Use training examples to establish behavior, then apply the stored model to held-out examples with known labels. Record each result rather than only a summary percentage. Compare kinds of mistakes and relevant conditions. After using a failure to guide a revision, reserve new examples for another check. This helps you distinguish correcting a known case from improving performance on unfamiliar cases.", "A rule-based chatbot provides a useful comparison. Its replies come from conditions written by a programmer rather than from training on the classifier's examples. It still processes language, and its phrase order, normalization, and fallback determine behavior. Document which messages you tested and which it misses. Keep all examples fictional, avoid collecting secrets, and provide a clear human help route. A limitation notice should describe the actual method and uncertainty rather than giving the system an exaggerated human personality.", "Document a dataset so another person can interpret it. Include the feature names, units where relevant, label definitions, collection method, intended task, and known gaps. Describe how examples were divided into training and evaluation sets. If a label is uncertain, explain the policy instead of hiding the disagreement. If examples represent only a narrow setting, say so in the model notice. A transparent dataset description does not guarantee a good model, but it helps a reviewer identify possible weaknesses and reproduce the investigation. It also prevents a future user from assuming that a tiny classroom dataset represents a broad real-world population."], "support": "Revisit this example: Imagine two adjacent objects in a 3 by 3 region map. Assign regions 1,2,4 to one cup and regions 6,8,9 to one bowl, leaving 3,5,7 as background. The region labels alone do not distinguish two different cups unless you also record object identifiers. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Design a diverse twelve-example training set. Explain one source of cultural or context bias and how the set addresses it. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w8d3", "week": 8, "day": 3, "title": "Audit model behavior", "standards": ["AI.ML.8.c", "AI.SI.8.a", "AI.SI.8.b", "DA.VC.8.a"], "reading": ["A model can reproduce patterns and gaps in its training data. If size is correlated with category only in a narrow sample, a size-only classifier may fail on unfamiliar objects. Bias can enter through collection, labeling, feature choice, or evaluation. More rows do not automatically solve a bad feature assumption.", "Evaluate using a held-out test set with known labels. Record the prediction for each example, count correct predictions, and examine the kinds of errors. Overall accuracy can hide poor performance in a small subgroup. Compare outcomes across relevant conditions rather than stopping at one percentage.", "Inclusive design includes input from affected users, suitable data, careful algorithm choices, and validation. Describe the intended use and a fallback when confidence or evidence is insufficient. Our classifier always returns a nearest label; it has no calibrated confidence score, so a confident-looking label should not be treated as certainty."], "example": "Held-out tests: size 1 cup, size 6 cup, size 9 bowl, size 10 bowl. With default training, size 6 is mislabeled while the other three are correct: 3/4=75%. That summary hides the failure on medium-size cups. A revised set should be evaluated on additional unseen examples, not just the corrected row.", "tasks": ["Evaluate the default model on the four held-out examples. Create a result table and calculate accuracy.", "Revise training examples to address one failure. Keep two additional examples aside for a fresh test; record the new results.", "Write an inclusive design audit addressing collection, labeling, feature choice, subgroup evaluation, and human fallback."], "exit": "Why should you test a revision on examples not used to make the revision?", "lab": "ai", "starter": "", "extension": ["Machine learning work requires an explicit task and evaluation plan. Decide whether the desired output is a category or a number, select relevant input features, and define what a correct output means. A nearest-neighbor classifier can use numeric distance to select a category, but distance in one feature is only a useful shortcut if that feature relates to the task. Size alone cannot reliably capture every distinction between cups and bowls.", "A data set includes choices made by people. Which examples were collected, which categories were defined, how ambiguous examples were labeled, and which conditions were omitted all affect behavior. Repeating many similar examples may increase the row count while leaving important gaps. Adding diverse examples can help, but it does not automatically correct an unsuitable feature, inconsistent labels, or a poorly chosen evaluation measure.", "Separate model development from evaluation. Use training examples to establish behavior, then apply the stored model to held-out examples with known labels. Record each result rather than only a summary percentage. Compare kinds of mistakes and relevant conditions. After using a failure to guide a revision, reserve new examples for another check. This helps you distinguish correcting a known case from improving performance on unfamiliar cases.", "A rule-based chatbot provides a useful comparison. Its replies come from conditions written by a programmer rather than from training on the classifier's examples. It still processes language, and its phrase order, normalization, and fallback determine behavior. Document which messages you tested and which it misses. Keep all examples fictional, avoid collecting secrets, and provide a clear human help route. A limitation notice should describe the actual method and uncertainty rather than giving the system an exaggerated human personality.", "Document a dataset so another person can interpret it. Include the feature names, units where relevant, label definitions, collection method, intended task, and known gaps. Describe how examples were divided into training and evaluation sets. If a label is uncertain, explain the policy instead of hiding the disagreement. If examples represent only a narrow setting, say so in the model notice. A transparent dataset description does not guarantee a good model, but it helps a reviewer identify possible weaknesses and reproduce the investigation. It also prevents a future user from assuming that a tiny classroom dataset represents a broad real-world population."], "support": "Revisit this example: Held-out tests: size 1 cup, size 6 cup, size 9 bowl, size 10 bowl. With default training, size 6 is mislabeled while the other three are correct: 3/4=75%. That summary hides the failure on medium-size cups. A revised set should be evaluated on additional unseen examples, not just the corrected row. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write an inclusive design audit addressing collection, labeling, feature choice, subgroup evaluation, and human fallback. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w8d4", "week": 8, "day": 4, "title": "Program a functional help bot", "standards": ["AI.NI.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.c", "IC.SLE.8.d"], "reading": ["A chatbot accepts a message and produces a response. A simple language-processing algorithm can normalize text, match a phrase or keyword, and select a reply. This is a functional chatbot even when it does not use a trained language model. Explain its actual method rather than presenting it as human understanding.", "Decompose the bot into normalization, matching, and response selection. Parameters let the same reply function accept different messages. A fallback prevents an unknown request from receiving an unrelated answer. Check specific phrases before broad keywords and avoid promising actions the bot cannot perform.", "Collaborative programming includes both contribution and review. One student can draft rules while another tests edge cases, then switch roles digitally. If working independently, write a second-role review. Credit the starter and any partner contributions. Never ask users for passwords or personal records in a classroom bot."], "example": "function reply(message){const t=message.toLowerCase(); if(t.includes(\"reset password\")) return \"Ask your teacher through a trusted channel.\"; if(t.includes(\"hello\")) return \"Hello!\"; return \"I do not recognize that request.\";} The bot processes text and returns a response; it does not reset an account.", "tasks": ["Plan at least four helpful intents and a safe fallback in Chatbot Lab on the earlier context lesson.", "Implement the rules in JavaScript here with a reply(message) procedure. Test ordinary, uppercase, paraphrased, overlapping, and unknown messages.", "Ask a partner to test or complete a second-role review. Revise one rule, credit contributions, and submit code plus test evidence."], "exit": "Why must the bot describe a real help route rather than claim it changed an account?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "function reply(message) {\n const t=message.toLowerCase();\n if(t.includes(\"reset password\")) return \"Ask your teacher through a trusted channel.\";\n if(t.includes(\"hello\")) return \"Hello!\";\n return \"I do not recognize that request.\";\n}\nfor(const m of [\"HELLO\",\"reset password\",\"unknown\"]) print(reply(m));", "extension": ["Machine learning work requires an explicit task and evaluation plan. Decide whether the desired output is a category or a number, select relevant input features, and define what a correct output means. A nearest-neighbor classifier can use numeric distance to select a category, but distance in one feature is only a useful shortcut if that feature relates to the task. Size alone cannot reliably capture every distinction between cups and bowls.", "A data set includes choices made by people. Which examples were collected, which categories were defined, how ambiguous examples were labeled, and which conditions were omitted all affect behavior. Repeating many similar examples may increase the row count while leaving important gaps. Adding diverse examples can help, but it does not automatically correct an unsuitable feature, inconsistent labels, or a poorly chosen evaluation measure.", "Separate model development from evaluation. Use training examples to establish behavior, then apply the stored model to held-out examples with known labels. Record each result rather than only a summary percentage. Compare kinds of mistakes and relevant conditions. After using a failure to guide a revision, reserve new examples for another check. This helps you distinguish correcting a known case from improving performance on unfamiliar cases.", "A rule-based chatbot provides a useful comparison. Its replies come from conditions written by a programmer rather than from training on the classifier's examples. It still processes language, and its phrase order, normalization, and fallback determine behavior. Document which messages you tested and which it misses. Keep all examples fictional, avoid collecting secrets, and provide a clear human help route. A limitation notice should describe the actual method and uncertainty rather than giving the system an exaggerated human personality.", "Document a dataset so another person can interpret it. Include the feature names, units where relevant, label definitions, collection method, intended task, and known gaps. Describe how examples were divided into training and evaluation sets. If a label is uncertain, explain the policy instead of hiding the disagreement. If examples represent only a narrow setting, say so in the model notice. A transparent dataset description does not guarantee a good model, but it helps a reviewer identify possible weaknesses and reproduce the investigation. It also prevents a future user from assuming that a tiny classroom dataset represents a broad real-world population."], "support": "Revisit this example: function reply(message){const t=message.toLowerCase(); if(t.includes(\"reset password\")) return \"Ask your teacher through a trusted channel.\"; if(t.includes(\"hello\")) return \"Hello!\"; return \"I do not recognize that request.\";} The bot processes text and returns a response; it does not reset an account. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Ask a partner to test or complete a second-role review. Revise one rule, credit contributions, and submit code plus test evidence. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w8d5", "week": 8, "day": 5, "title": "Demonstrate and document AI limits", "standards": ["AI.NI.8.a", "AI.ML.8.a", "AI.ML.8.c", "AI.SI.8.b"], "reading": ["An AI project report should explain the task, method, evidence, and limitations. A trained classifier and a rule-based chatbot solve different problems. The classifier uses stored examples to choose a category. The chatbot uses programmer-written language rules. Both can fail, and neither deserves trust merely because it produces an answer.", "A demonstration is strongest when it includes a failure case. Explain why the failure occurs, what revision you made, and which tests you reran. Do not hide a missed paraphrase or a misclassified example. An honest account helps a future user decide when to rely on the tool and when to seek help.", "Responsible use also includes privacy, accessibility, and scope. Keep examples fictional, provide readable text output, avoid unnecessary data collection, and identify a human fallback. Your teacher will use the weekly test for formal concept scores; your artifact and audit provide evidence of application."], "example": "A bot handles “hello” but misses “good morning.” Adding a greeting rule helps that case but does not prove understanding of all greetings. A report lists the tested messages, output, rule change, and remaining unknown inputs.", "tasks": ["Run a final five-case test of your chatbot and include a known limitation.", "Compare your chatbot method with the nearest-neighbor classifier. Explain training, inference, rule matching, and one failure for each.", "Submit bot code, classifier results, dataset audit, attribution, and a user-facing limitation notice. Complete the Week 8 test."], "exit": "What should a user know before relying on your bot's response?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["Machine learning work requires an explicit task and evaluation plan. Decide whether the desired output is a category or a number, select relevant input features, and define what a correct output means. A nearest-neighbor classifier can use numeric distance to select a category, but distance in one feature is only a useful shortcut if that feature relates to the task. Size alone cannot reliably capture every distinction between cups and bowls.", "A data set includes choices made by people. Which examples were collected, which categories were defined, how ambiguous examples were labeled, and which conditions were omitted all affect behavior. Repeating many similar examples may increase the row count while leaving important gaps. Adding diverse examples can help, but it does not automatically correct an unsuitable feature, inconsistent labels, or a poorly chosen evaluation measure.", "Separate model development from evaluation. Use training examples to establish behavior, then apply the stored model to held-out examples with known labels. Record each result rather than only a summary percentage. Compare kinds of mistakes and relevant conditions. After using a failure to guide a revision, reserve new examples for another check. This helps you distinguish correcting a known case from improving performance on unfamiliar cases.", "A rule-based chatbot provides a useful comparison. Its replies come from conditions written by a programmer rather than from training on the classifier's examples. It still processes language, and its phrase order, normalization, and fallback determine behavior. Document which messages you tested and which it misses. Keep all examples fictional, avoid collecting secrets, and provide a clear human help route. A limitation notice should describe the actual method and uncertainty rather than giving the system an exaggerated human personality.", "Document a dataset so another person can interpret it. Include the feature names, units where relevant, label definitions, collection method, intended task, and known gaps. Describe how examples were divided into training and evaluation sets. If a label is uncertain, explain the policy instead of hiding the disagreement. If examples represent only a narrow setting, say so in the model notice. A transparent dataset description does not guarantee a good model, but it helps a reviewer identify possible weaknesses and reproduce the investigation. It also prevents a future user from assuming that a tiny classroom dataset represents a broad real-world population."], "support": "Revisit this example: A bot handles “hello” but misses “good morning.” Adding a greeting rule helps that case but does not prove understanding of all greetings. A report lists the tested messages, output, rule change, and remaining unknown inputs. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Submit bot code, classifier results, dataset audit, attribution, and a user-facing limitation notice. Complete the Week 8 test. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}, {"title": "Design for people and present evidence", "goal": "Create, test, revise, and communicate a browser-based solution with privacy and accessibility in mind.", "lessons": [{"id": "w9d1", "week": 9, "day": 1, "title": "Choose a problem worth solving", "standards": ["IC.Cu.8.a", "IC.Cu.8.c", "IC.Cu.8.d", "ATP.M.8.a"], "reading": ["A capstone brings together technical choices and human needs. Choose a fictional school or community task: a temperature alert report, a learning help bot, or a virtual robot route planner. Define the user, the problem, the required output, and constraints. Keep the scope small enough to implement and test in this week.", "Computing can change jobs and economic activity. Automation may reduce repetitive work while creating new responsibilities for maintenance, analysis, accessibility, and security. Compare benefits and costs rather than assuming every automated solution improves every person's experience.", "Different careers contribute to a project. A developer writes behavior, a designer studies usability, a tester investigates failures, and a data specialist evaluates information quality. You can perform these roles in a small classroom project. Explain which role interests you and what evidence from the course supports that interest."], "example": "Problem: a fictional greenhouse team needs readable alerts from temperature data. Required output: count of readings at or above a chosen threshold, a labeled graph, and a clear warning about missing data. Constraints: browser only, fictional data, keyboard access, and a five-minute demonstration.", "tasks": ["Choose a capstone and write user, problem, requirements, constraints, and success criteria.", "Decompose it into at least three subproblems and assign a plan for each day.", "Compare two computing careers involved and two economic effects of the proposed technology. Use course readings as sources and identify further research questions."], "exit": "Which requirement will you be able to test objectively?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["A finished computational project connects a user need to a working artifact and evidence. Requirements define observable success; constraints define limits on the design. Write both clearly enough that a reviewer can determine whether they were met. A statement such as easy to use needs a specific task and test, such as completing the main action with a keyboard and understanding every output label.", "Manage scope by protecting the core function first. Save a working version before adding an optional feature. Decompose a complicated task into modules, then test their connections. Document missing inputs and failure behavior as well as normal output. If a planned feature cannot be completed, identify the unmet requirement and its effect on the user rather than disguising it with a polished presentation.", "Review should produce actionable evidence. Give another person a task and observe where instructions, labels, or behavior cause difficulty. A digital comment should identify the location, the observed issue, and a useful next step. If you review your own work, adopt a specific user role and state that it was a self-review. Do not claim external testing that did not occur. Revise, then repeat the affected checks.", "Present the project with proportionate claims. Show the problem, method, successful case, challenging case, revision, and remaining limitation. Include credits for reused code, text, data, media, and collaboration. Explain privacy and accessibility choices in terms of their effect on people. A strong reflection identifies a principle that transfers to another problem, such as testing boundaries or collecting only needed data, and a question that would guide the next development cycle.", "A final submission should be usable without your spoken explanation. Include a short start instruction, the artifact, example inputs, expected outputs, test evidence, revision notes, and credits. Use names that reveal purpose and version so the teacher can locate the intended final work. Check that the submitted file opens and contains the latest changes. A local save, a download, and a Classroom turn-in are different actions; confirm the last step in the official assignment. If a limitation remains, state its practical effect and a sensible next test. This makes your work reviewable and gives the next developer a clear starting point."], "support": "Revisit this example: Problem: a fictional greenhouse team needs readable alerts from temperature data. Required output: count of readings at or above a chosen threshold, a labeled graph, and a clear warning about missing data. Constraints: browser only, fictional data, keyboard access, and a five-minute demonstration. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Compare two computing careers involved and two economic effects of the proposed technology. Use course readings as sources and identify further research questions. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w9d2", "week": 9, "day": 2, "title": "Privacy and communication by design", "standards": ["IC.SLE.8.a", "IC.SLE.8.b", "IC.SLE.8.c", "IC.SI.8.a"], "reading": ["A design can collect information even when users do not type it directly. Logs, timestamps, location, and device identifiers may reveal behavior. A privacy review asks what is collected, why it is needed, where it is stored, who can access it, and when it should be removed. Avoid unnecessary collection in your capstone.", "Privacy decisions have social and economic consequences. A convenient shared file can expose personal details if access is too broad. A strict access control can protect data but create support needs. An ethical design explains the tradeoff and provides a reasonable help route. This classroom exercise is a design analysis, not legal advice about a real organization.", "Electronic communication supports asynchronous work and distant collaboration, but tone and misunderstandings can be harder to resolve. Face-to-face discussion offers immediate feedback and nonverbal cues but requires participants to be available together. Choose a channel based on the task and people, and use respectful, specific feedback."], "example": "A digital review comment says “Your graph has no units; please label degrees Celsius.” It is specific and actionable. “This is bad” gives little guidance. A sensitive disagreement might be better discussed with teacher support rather than repeated in public comments.", "tasks": ["Create a capstone data-flow and privacy table: field, purpose, storage, access, retention, and risk.", "Compare a digital peer review with a face-to-face review for your project. Explain one benefit and one limitation of each.", "Write two respectful review comments and revise your project to reduce unnecessary collection or sharing."], "exit": "What information might a system generate without a user explicitly typing it?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["A finished computational project connects a user need to a working artifact and evidence. Requirements define observable success; constraints define limits on the design. Write both clearly enough that a reviewer can determine whether they were met. A statement such as easy to use needs a specific task and test, such as completing the main action with a keyboard and understanding every output label.", "Manage scope by protecting the core function first. Save a working version before adding an optional feature. Decompose a complicated task into modules, then test their connections. Document missing inputs and failure behavior as well as normal output. If a planned feature cannot be completed, identify the unmet requirement and its effect on the user rather than disguising it with a polished presentation.", "Review should produce actionable evidence. Give another person a task and observe where instructions, labels, or behavior cause difficulty. A digital comment should identify the location, the observed issue, and a useful next step. If you review your own work, adopt a specific user role and state that it was a self-review. Do not claim external testing that did not occur. Revise, then repeat the affected checks.", "Present the project with proportionate claims. Show the problem, method, successful case, challenging case, revision, and remaining limitation. Include credits for reused code, text, data, media, and collaboration. Explain privacy and accessibility choices in terms of their effect on people. A strong reflection identifies a principle that transfers to another problem, such as testing boundaries or collecting only needed data, and a question that would guide the next development cycle.", "A final submission should be usable without your spoken explanation. Include a short start instruction, the artifact, example inputs, expected outputs, test evidence, revision notes, and credits. Use names that reveal purpose and version so the teacher can locate the intended final work. Check that the submitted file opens and contains the latest changes. A local save, a download, and a Classroom turn-in are different actions; confirm the last step in the official assignment. If a limitation remains, state its practical effect and a sensible next test. This makes your work reviewable and gives the next developer a clear starting point."], "support": "Revisit this example: A digital review comment says “Your graph has no units; please label degrees Celsius.” It is specific and actionable. “This is bad” gives little guidance. A sensitive disagreement might be better discussed with teacher support rather than repeated in public comments. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Write two respectful review comments and revise your project to reduce unnecessary collection or sharing. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w9d3", "week": 9, "day": 3, "title": "Build and test your artifact", "standards": ["ATP.PD.8.a", "ATP.PD.8.b", "ATP.PD.8.c", "DA.VC.8.b"], "reading": ["Implementation turns a plan into an artifact that another person can use. Your capstone must include an actual computational component: a program with inputs and outputs, a spreadsheet with formulas and a graph, or a documented robot program. A poster describing what software could do is not a substitute for working behavior.", "Use a test matrix tied to requirements. Include ordinary cases, boundary cases, an empty or missing input, and an unusual valid case. For each, predict the output before running. If a requirement is accessibility, test keyboard use and readable labels as well as the numeric result.", "When time is limited, reduce optional features instead of removing the evidence for core requirements. Preserve a working version before making changes. A small correct and well-explained artifact is easier to review than a large unfinished one. Record any requirement you did not meet."], "example": "A threshold report accepts a collection and a limit, counts matching readings, and prints a result. Test [] at 24, [23,24,25] at 24, and [24] at 24. The outputs should match the chosen at-least requirement and the documented empty-data policy.", "tasks": ["Implement your chosen capstone. Use an array, loop, decision, and parameterized procedure if coding; formulas and a labeled graph if using Sheets.", "Run at least five planned tests and record expected and actual outputs. Link each test to a requirement.", "Repair a defect, rerun earlier tests, and save a versioned artifact. Record remaining limitations."], "exit": "Which test gives the strongest evidence that your core requirement works?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["A finished computational project connects a user need to a working artifact and evidence. Requirements define observable success; constraints define limits on the design. Write both clearly enough that a reviewer can determine whether they were met. A statement such as easy to use needs a specific task and test, such as completing the main action with a keyboard and understanding every output label.", "Manage scope by protecting the core function first. Save a working version before adding an optional feature. Decompose a complicated task into modules, then test their connections. Document missing inputs and failure behavior as well as normal output. If a planned feature cannot be completed, identify the unmet requirement and its effect on the user rather than disguising it with a polished presentation.", "Review should produce actionable evidence. Give another person a task and observe where instructions, labels, or behavior cause difficulty. A digital comment should identify the location, the observed issue, and a useful next step. If you review your own work, adopt a specific user role and state that it was a self-review. Do not claim external testing that did not occur. Revise, then repeat the affected checks.", "Present the project with proportionate claims. Show the problem, method, successful case, challenging case, revision, and remaining limitation. Include credits for reused code, text, data, media, and collaboration. Explain privacy and accessibility choices in terms of their effect on people. A strong reflection identifies a principle that transfers to another problem, such as testing boundaries or collecting only needed data, and a question that would guide the next development cycle.", "A final submission should be usable without your spoken explanation. Include a short start instruction, the artifact, example inputs, expected outputs, test evidence, revision notes, and credits. Use names that reveal purpose and version so the teacher can locate the intended final work. Check that the submitted file opens and contains the latest changes. A local save, a download, and a Classroom turn-in are different actions; confirm the last step in the official assignment. If a limitation remains, state its practical effect and a sensible next test. This makes your work reviewable and gives the next developer a clear starting point."], "support": "Revisit this example: A threshold report accepts a collection and a limit, counts matching readings, and prints a result. Test [] at 24, [23,24,25] at 24, and [24] at 24. The outputs should match the chosen at-least requirement and the documented empty-data policy. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Repair a defect, rerun earlier tests, and save a versioned artifact. Record remaining limitations. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w9d4", "week": 9, "day": 4, "title": "Review revise and give credit", "standards": ["IC.Cu.8.b", "IC.SLE.8.d", "ATP.PD.8.b"], "reading": ["A review is a chance to improve a project using another perspective. Ask the reviewer to complete a specific task, describe what happened, and identify a barrier. Record evidence instead of defending the first design. If no partner is available, use a defined user role and test the project against that role's needs.", "Accessibility review includes text size, contrast, keyboard access, clear labels, and alternatives to information conveyed only by sound or color. Inclusive review also asks who is missing from the dataset or assumptions. One successful test with one user cannot establish universal accessibility.", "Credit sources and contributions. Record creator, title, location, and how you used the material. Permission and attribution are different: naming a creator does not automatically grant permission to reuse protected work. Use your own material, permitted resources, or school-approved sources. Include the course starter when you adapt it."], "example": "Credit entry: CS Innovation Academy, countAbove classroom starter, Week 5 lesson 4; adapted the parameterized counting procedure and added input validation. Partner contribution: tested boundary inputs and suggested clearer labels. This identifies what was reused and who helped.", "tasks": ["Ask a partner to run your artifact or perform a documented second-role review. Record three observations and one accessibility barrier.", "Revise one behavior and one explanation. Rerun affected tests and compare before and after.", "Prepare a credits list for code, text, data, images, and collaboration. Explain any reuse permissions relevant to external materials."], "exit": "Why is attribution different from permission?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["A finished computational project connects a user need to a working artifact and evidence. Requirements define observable success; constraints define limits on the design. Write both clearly enough that a reviewer can determine whether they were met. A statement such as easy to use needs a specific task and test, such as completing the main action with a keyboard and understanding every output label.", "Manage scope by protecting the core function first. Save a working version before adding an optional feature. Decompose a complicated task into modules, then test their connections. Document missing inputs and failure behavior as well as normal output. If a planned feature cannot be completed, identify the unmet requirement and its effect on the user rather than disguising it with a polished presentation.", "Review should produce actionable evidence. Give another person a task and observe where instructions, labels, or behavior cause difficulty. A digital comment should identify the location, the observed issue, and a useful next step. If you review your own work, adopt a specific user role and state that it was a self-review. Do not claim external testing that did not occur. Revise, then repeat the affected checks.", "Present the project with proportionate claims. Show the problem, method, successful case, challenging case, revision, and remaining limitation. Include credits for reused code, text, data, media, and collaboration. Explain privacy and accessibility choices in terms of their effect on people. A strong reflection identifies a principle that transfers to another problem, such as testing boundaries or collecting only needed data, and a question that would guide the next development cycle.", "A final submission should be usable without your spoken explanation. Include a short start instruction, the artifact, example inputs, expected outputs, test evidence, revision notes, and credits. Use names that reveal purpose and version so the teacher can locate the intended final work. Check that the submitted file opens and contains the latest changes. A local save, a download, and a Classroom turn-in are different actions; confirm the last step in the official assignment. If a limitation remains, state its practical effect and a sensible next test. This makes your work reviewable and gives the next developer a clear starting point."], "support": "Revisit this example: Credit entry: CS Innovation Academy, countAbove classroom starter, Week 5 lesson 4; adapted the parameterized counting procedure and added input validation. Partner contribution: tested boundary inputs and suggested clearer labels. This identifies what was reused and who helped. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Prepare a credits list for code, text, data, images, and collaboration. Explain any reuse permissions relevant to external materials. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}, {"id": "w9d5", "week": 9, "day": 5, "title": "Present what the evidence shows", "standards": ["IC.Cu.8.d", "IC.SLE.8.d", "ATP.PD.8.b"], "reading": ["A technical presentation explains the problem, demonstrates the artifact, and shows why the result deserves a stated level of trust. Begin with the user's need. Show one normal case and one challenging case. Present a revision with before-and-after evidence. State a limitation that matters for actual use.", "Avoid overstating what you tested. If you ran a toy robot model, say that the route succeeds under the model's rules. If you tested a classifier on six objects, report that test set instead of claiming universal accuracy. If a platform import has not been tested, say so. Precise scope makes the presentation more useful.", "Reflection identifies a transferable idea and a next question. You might explain how systematic testing helped both troubleshooting and coding, or how privacy affected both networks and AI. Complete the final assessment assigned by your teacher and submit the artifact, test evidence, review, and credits through Classroom."], "example": "Presentation statement: My report correctly flagged the three tested boundary values after I changed > to >=. The model does not account for sensor error or delayed readings. Before real use, it would need additional tests and an appropriate human response plan.", "tasks": ["Prepare a five-part digital presentation: problem and user, working demonstration, test evidence, revision, and limitations with credits.", "Submit your final artifact, five-case test matrix, review notes, and reflection in Classroom. Explain one connection across two course units.", "Complete the Week 9 test and quarter final on your teacher's schedule. Identify one skill you can use in a new problem and one next learning goal."], "exit": "What claim can you make confidently, and what evidence defines its limits?", "lab": "coding", "starter": "", "extension": ["A finished computational project connects a user need to a working artifact and evidence. Requirements define observable success; constraints define limits on the design. Write both clearly enough that a reviewer can determine whether they were met. A statement such as easy to use needs a specific task and test, such as completing the main action with a keyboard and understanding every output label.", "Manage scope by protecting the core function first. Save a working version before adding an optional feature. Decompose a complicated task into modules, then test their connections. Document missing inputs and failure behavior as well as normal output. If a planned feature cannot be completed, identify the unmet requirement and its effect on the user rather than disguising it with a polished presentation.", "Review should produce actionable evidence. Give another person a task and observe where instructions, labels, or behavior cause difficulty. A digital comment should identify the location, the observed issue, and a useful next step. If you review your own work, adopt a specific user role and state that it was a self-review. Do not claim external testing that did not occur. Revise, then repeat the affected checks.", "Present the project with proportionate claims. Show the problem, method, successful case, challenging case, revision, and remaining limitation. Include credits for reused code, text, data, media, and collaboration. Explain privacy and accessibility choices in terms of their effect on people. A strong reflection identifies a principle that transfers to another problem, such as testing boundaries or collecting only needed data, and a question that would guide the next development cycle.", "A final submission should be usable without your spoken explanation. Include a short start instruction, the artifact, example inputs, expected outputs, test evidence, revision notes, and credits. Use names that reveal purpose and version so the teacher can locate the intended final work. Check that the submitted file opens and contains the latest changes. A local save, a download, and a Classroom turn-in are different actions; confirm the last step in the official assignment. If a limitation remains, state its practical effect and a sensible next test. This makes your work reviewable and gives the next developer a clear starting point."], "support": "Revisit this example: Presentation statement: My report correctly flagged the three tested boundary values after I changed > to >=. The model does not account for sensor error or delayed readings. Before real use, it would need additional tests and an appropriate human response plan. Write each input, action, and result separately. Then complete only the first investigation task and compare it with the example before proceeding.", "stretch": "Extend this lesson: Complete the Week 9 test and quarter final on your teacher's schedule. Identify one skill you can use in a new problem and one next learning goal. Add one new constraint, predict its effect, and show a test that could distinguish your revised design from the original."}]}];